Contact: untitled.movie

[[Workshop draft — English service translation of the interwoven book (verwebung/Verwebung.fountain, as of 2 September 2026): 142 scenes in the author's current order. The 86 TAU scenes follow the existing English translation of the first draft (drehbuch-v1-en.fountain, as of 11 August 2026), with the weave's own changes applied: the dictation without the name, the Lenz voice in "Sentences Side by Side", the hard cut after the pretitle night, no end card after the run. The 56 scenes of The Fields are translated afresh from the German working text. The German master's workshop notes are not carried over; the colour notes at the end of each scene heading (blue = TAU, orange = The Fields) are Beat card colours and do not print, and the GREY/COLOUR labels of the sections are workshop convention only — both strands are in colour. The author's revision happens in the German master.]]


# 1. GREY · TAU, Seq 1 "The Night" / Seq 2 "The Home" (G01–G06)

BLACK.

The sound of a house at night: wood working. Wind in a chimney. Very far away: a dog.

.INT. HIDEOUT, LIVING ROOM — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The World of Undersides" · "The Knock" · "The Arm in the Light" · "Two Shots" · [G01 · TAU Seq 1]

Floorboards, very close. A strip of light lies across them and does not tremble.

We are at floor level, under a table. Of the world there are only undersides: chair legs. The hem of a curtain. A kerosene lamp standing on the floor, throwing a yellow circle. In the circle, among the things: a small glass vial with a beautiful stopper.

Two voices whisper, a woman, a man. No words, only sound — the sound of people who have spoken quietly for years and are good at it.

Then, for the length of one breath, the woman's whisper becomes language:

MOTHER (O.S.)
(whispering)
... high above everything a place. You take the path that turns...

A KNOCK.

The whispering stops.

A bolt. A door opens.

FATHER (O.S.)
(loud — loud for the first time)
... !

NOTE ON SOUND: The father has seen who is standing there, and the first moment is a good one: he says a single word — a name. It does not sound like fear. It sounds like recognition, almost like joy that should know better. The word is spoken, not slurred; but room, furniture and heartbeat lie between us: it does not come through. Not on first viewing, not on second. Only in the third return of the night (scene 55) does it become almost language — two syllables.

A man's legs in the doorframe: work boots, wet trouser hems that stop dripping. A voice — too deep to hold words — breaks. Starts again. Breaks.

Then an arm stretches into the light of the lamp. In the hand: a gun. Above the wrist, where the sleeve slides back, a dark sign on the skin. Small. Shaped like the shadowy head of a horse.

The child under the table: four years old, a face in the half-dark. It looks at the sign.

The first SHOT: only a jolt of the light and a fall. We are on the child's face and we stay there.

At the second, something throws itself between — a skirt, a familiar fabric. The floor shudders. Once.

Then the world stands still. The boots stay. Longer than boots are allowed to stay. A sound, perhaps a whimper.

Then they are gone.

The lamp keeps burning. The yellow circle. The vial still stands.


BLACK.

> TITLE: [untitled] <

> HARD CUT:


.INT. DORMITORY, THE HOME — PRE-DAWN [[blue]]

= "Listening to the House" · [G02 · TAU Seq 2]

A face, fourteen years old, awake. ANNA.

No title card. No transition. Whoever saw the four-year-old must make the leap themselves — and makes it on this face.

A dormitory, thirty beds. All the sleepers about the same age, fourteen, fifteen — as if someone had locked up an entire school and forgotten the years before and after.

Anna lies still, eyes open, and listens to the house:

A floorboard creaks, far away. Her eyes follow. A key, two doors down. Her eyes follow. A cough, a second one — she knows before we do which bed the third will come from.


.EXT. YARD, THE HOME / SKY — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Drops" · [G03 · TAU Seq 2]

A SIREN. Doors slam, thirty children run into the yard as if they had never done anything else.

In the sky: a helicopter drops padded crates. The DROPS.

The crates hit the ground. The children are there first, opening, sorting, carrying — a practiced system with rules nobody explains.

The GROWN-UPS arrive last: few, grey, thin, all in long sleeves despite the sun.


.EXT./INT. YARD / STOREROOM — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Sixteen" · [G04 · TAU Seq 2]

The storeroom: shelves with a few Drops rations, two lists on a nail.

The last two of the ten crates are set down by two children. A HOUSEPARENT (50s, fleshy face, quick hands) counts them off with his chin. Arrogant and disdainful, he watches them.

On one crate, sticking to the underside: a SLIP OF PAPER. Cleanly printed, from over there — a leftover from the packing that nobody over there removed. Beneath signs that are nobody's business here, a number:

> 16 <

The houseparent sees the slip. Tears it off in passing, without changing stride, and lets it vanish into his pocket. Quick hands.

Anna sees it.


.EXT. YARD, THE HOME — LATER [[blue]]

= "Half the Ration" · [G05 · TAU Seq 2]

At the edge of the yard. A BOY plants himself in front of Anna, half a loaf of bread in his hand. He holds it out, half offer, half question.

BOY
The sugar.

Anna looks at the bread. Then at him.

ANNA
The room behind the coal chute. Third crate. Under the empty ones.

The bread changes owners. The boy leaves.

Anna eats half. The other half she wraps up and hides — behind a loose brick.


.INT. CLASSROOM, THE HOME — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Dictation" · [G06 · TAU Seq 2]

Thirty lowered heads. A MATRON (60s, grey, long sleeves) dictates in a voice that has dictated this too many times:

MATRON
... destroyed by the terrorist attack — comma — which the traitor committed against his own city — full stop.

On the wall: a yellowed poster. A vast building, proud, seen from below; in front of it, small, four people with flags. Three of the faces someone has scratched out with a pencil — years ago, the grey has long gone shiny. The fourth nobody has touched: a young woman, a child on her arm.

MATRON (CONT'D)
From justice he removed himself, a casualty of his own deed.

Anna takes the dictation neatly. Beautiful, even handwriting. Her face says nothing.

[[Weave-only change (1 Sept 2026): the dictation no longer names Jahn — it should read as distant schoolwork until the communiqué (F20) turns it into propaganda in retrospect.]]



# 2. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq I "The Fields" (F1–F3)

.F1 — COLOUR [[orange]]

= "Colour" · [F1 · The Fields Seq I]

So much colour that the eyes have to sort it first: fields to the horizon, shoulder-high stalks, in bloom. People in rows between them, bent, with knives. Over everything a scent you seem to be able to see — as if the air stood a little denser above the rows.

By the field track a POSTER on two posts, fresh: a painted flower, and a sign, curved like a signature — the Greek letter τ. Beneath it, elegant: TAU.

CARTS roll along the track, piled high with flower heads, one behind the other, in behind a yard wall. Above the gate the same sign, in iron.


.F1a — THE FLACON IN THE WINDOW [[orange]]

= "The Flacon in the Window" · [F1a · The Fields Seq I]

INT. SHOP, CITY — DAY

A shop window with a single display: a FLACON, smaller than a thumb, on velvet, under a glass dome. In front of it a child, nose to the glass. Behind the child, mirrored in the window, the carts roll past — wagon after wagon for this one little bottle.

The child is pulled away. The flacon stays.


.F2 — THREE HOURS [[orange]]

= "Three Hours" · [F2 · The Fields Seq I]

EXT. EDGE OF THE FIELD — DAY

At the end of a row stands a young man in CITY SHOES, which are wrong here. PAUL JAHN, late twenties, a face that can listen. Under his arm a folder. He waits the way a man waits who knows he is in the way.

A FOREMAN comes down the row, in no hurry: LENZ, thirty, hands like rootwood. He looks Jahn over the way you look over a tool that is no good.

LENZ
You're the one they told me about.

JAHN
I was told you know the plant.

LENZ
Everyone here knows the plant.

JAHN
I was told: you know it with your hands. I only know it from papers.

Lenz looks at the folder. Then at Jahn again. He waits for the question everyone asks — when it blooms, how much it yields.

JAHN (CONT'D)
The analyses vary. Same variety, same field — and the values lie apart as if they were two plants. I don't think the analyses are wrong.
(Beat)
I think it depends on when you cut.

Lenz says nothing for a while. Then he does something he has never done for an educated man: he answers.

LENZ
Come.

They walk the rows.

LENZ
The row by the ditch first. It gets the sun from half past five, by eight it's done. Cut it at noon and you're cutting straw.
(walking, without turning round)
This one takes noon, because of the shade. That one over there doesn't. It holds a grudge — not right away. Three days later the blossom is dull, and nobody knows what from.

JAHN
(writing as he walks)
And none of this is written down.

LENZ
Where would it be written. It's written in the rows.
(he stops, bends a flower head aside, lets it spring back)
Cut too early, it's water. Too late, it's hay. In between there's an hour. Some days half an hour.

JAHN
How do you tell?

LENZ
When the row smells as if it's already been cut. Then.

Jahn takes notes. Then he stops writing and only listens.

LENZ (CONT'D)
The workers don't care. Neither does the factory. They pay by the kilo. A kilo at four is a kilo at two.
(Beat)
Except it isn't.

He stops. Breaks off a blossom — from a plant standing in the shade, still damp. Breaks a second one, a little further on, out of the sun. Crushes one in his left palm, the other in his right. Holds both hands out to Jahn.

LENZ (CONT'D)
Don't smell. Feel.

Jahn lays two fingers in the left hand, then in the right. On one, a film that sticks. On the other, one that draws like oil.

JAHN
It's — a difference.

LENZ
A difference.
(he laughs, short)
That's day and night, Mr Jahn. That's half the plant.

Jahn looks at his fingers. For him it's subtle. For Lenz it's glaring.

In montage we see the two of them move through the field: workers are introduced, the harvest is shown.

The sun stands differently by the time they reach the end of the last row. Three hours.

By the track Lenz ties three small bundles: stalks, separated by the hour of cutting, each marked with a straw — one knot, two knots, three.

LENZ (CONT'D)
Four o'clock. Seven o'clock. Noon.
(he presses them into Jahn's hand)
Don't boil them together.

Jahn takes them the way you take something worth more than it looks.


.F3 — THE NOSE [[orange]]

= "The Nose" · [F3 · The Fields Seq I]

INT. DISTILLERY, STILL HALL — DAY

A long brick building, tall windows, copper. STILLS in a row, each with a pipe that winds; beneath them fire, tended. Men in aprons, a rhythm of flaps, stirring, waiting.

The MASTER, sixty, stands at the third still and looks into the sight glass. He is not satisfied and does not know why.

Lenz comes in — here he is not the foreman, here he is the nose. The master steps aside without a word. Lenz lifts the lid of the sample cock, holds his hand over the mash, wafts once, draws the air in through his nose.

Once.

LENZ
Too hot. By a hair.

MASTER
The thermometer says —

LENZ
The thermometer can't smell.

The master looks at Lenz. Then he turns the valve — a quarter turn. No more.

Two WORKERS at the next still have been watching. One nods to the other.

Lenz is already leaving. At the door he wipes his hands on his trousers.



# 3. GREY · TAU, Seq 2 "The Home" (G07–G11)

.INT. STOREROOM — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Jacket" · [G07 · TAU Seq 2]

His jacket hangs over the edge of a crate while the houseparent stacks. Anna walks past; once, her hand brushes the jacket. It looks like nothing.


.INT. THE CARETAKER'S ROOM — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Almost-Mark" · [G08 · TAU Seq 2]

Anna walks along a corridor; from a small room comes a whimpering.

A narrow room, a bed, a smell of cold smoke. The CARETAKER (old, grey) lies in the corner.

CARETAKER
Water,

Anna does not hesitate long. Beside the caretaker stands a small cup. She takes it, goes out and comes back. She hands it to the sick man.

As he drinks, his sleeve slides back. A strip of skin above the wrist: something dark, several of them. We are too far away to see shapes.

Anna is not. Her gaze rests on the strip of skin, two seconds, three — then the cup is empty, the sleeve is down, her gaze is elsewhere.

She disappears without a word.


.INT. NOOK UNDER THE STAIRS, THE HOME — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Notebook" · [G09 · TAU Seq 2]

Anna's place: a niche under the stairs, with a view of two escape routes.

She has the dictation in front of her — and turns the page over. On the back she draws, from memory, with a hard pencil: a small sign. The one from the caretaker's wrist.

She looks at it. She compares it to nothing that we see.

Then she crosses it out — one stroke, clean, final.

Then we see THE NOTEBOOK whole: a thick, self-bound thing made of other people's paper — sheets printed on one side, dictations, forms, all written on from the back. Pages upon pages of small signs, every one crossed out.

At the very front a first page, older than everything else, soft-worn at the edge.

Anna leafs past it without opening it.


.EXT. HOME ADMINISTRATION, WINDOW — EVENING [[blue]]

= "The Nail" · [G10 · TAU Seq 2]

Anna passes outside the open window of the administration — for a moment there is a slip of paper in her hand, then there is none. You can see it. You do not have to.


.INT. DORMITORY — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Mother's Story" · [G11 · TAU Seq 2]

Anna starts out of sleep. No sound. Only her breath, too fast, and her hand already on the bed frame before she is awake.

We do not see the dream.

She lies back, stares into the dark. Then her lips begin to move — soundless, practiced. We hear scraps, less than a whisper:

ANNA
(barely audible)
... high above everything ... the whole city ... and in the evening comes the —

She stops.

She starts again, reaches the same place, stops.

Then, instead of going on, she begins to count. Her lips form numbers, soundless, one after another, the way other people count sheep.

That is how she falls asleep.



# 4. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq I "The Fields" (F4–F5a)

.F4 — BETWEEN TWO SIPS [[orange]]

= "Between Two Sips" · [F4 · The Fields Seq I]

INT. KITCHEN, KESSLER HOUSE — EVENING

A kitchen too small for the people in it. Twelve, fourteen people around a table for six, on chairs, on the bench, on the windowsill. Bread, sausage, a jug making the rounds.

RUTH, broad-built, hair pinned up, stands at the stove and laughs — and you can hear it, that is no figure of speech, two houses away. A child, ANTON, one year old, sleeps in the next room through all of it.

Jahn sits at the end of the table, where you seat guests you don't know yet. He has pushed the city shoes under his chair.

Lenz is telling a story. A routine about the master and the apprentice he once was — he plays both: the master with hunched shoulders, the apprentice with big eyes.

LENZ
(shoulders up, the master's official tone)
"The heads go out. They make you blind."
(eyes wide — now he is the apprentice, sixteen)
"And what do I do with the tails, master?"
(shoulders)
"Out as well. They make you stupid."

RUTH
(from the stove; she knows the routine, she feeds him the cue)
And the bit in between?

LENZ
(shoulders, dead serious)
"The bit in between makes you rich."
(eyes)
"Who, master? Who?"
(shoulders, a look down at the apprentice)
"Well now. Looks like somebody's been sneaking sips of the tails."

The table laughs. Ruth laughs loudest.

Jahn laughs longest. And most awkwardly — a beat too late, as if he had to work out the punchline first. Ruth sees it. She likes it.

RUTH
He laughs like someone who's still learning.

LENZ
He learns fast. He listened for three hours today without once saying what he knows.

RUTH
(to Jahn)
Then you're the first.

The jug reaches Lenz. He drinks. Sets it down. And says it casually, between two swallows, to nobody:

LENZ
Really the fields belong to the ones who know them.

He drinks on. At the table somebody is already talking about something else.

Only Jahn sets his glass down.

He says nothing. He looks at Lenz, who has already forgotten it. And you can watch a sentence find a place inside a man that it will never give up again.


.F5 — THE SHOCK [[orange]]

= "The Shock" · [F5 · The Fields Seq I]

INT. JAHN'S LABORATORY — NIGHT

No institute. A back room with shelves, glass, a small kettle on a burner. On the wall sheets with curves, tidy. On the table the three bundles: one knot, two knots, three.

Jahn works the way he always works: thoroughly, without haste. He puts the one-knot bundle into a vessel, seals it airtight — the routine of the perfume kitchen, everyone here boils the plant this way — and sets it on the burner. Notes the time.

LATER. The kettle is off the fire. Jahn lifts the lid, lets the vapours draw, waits. Then he reaches in, bare-handed, for the stalks, half cooled —

A JOLT.

Small. Sharp. Like a fence on a pasture.

He snatches his hand back. Looks at the hand. Looks at the vessel. Nothing burns, nothing smokes. A pot of plants.

He reaches in again. The jolt.

And again. On purpose this time, with one finger, slowly — and it comes, every time, out of the stalks themselves.

JAHN
(quietly)
What the hell.

He stands in front of the pot for a long time. Then he takes the three-knot bundle — noon — and sets it going: the same vessel, the same fire, the same time.

LATER. The noon stalks. He reaches in.

Nothing.

He looks over at the three bundles. One knot. Four o'clock.


.F5a — THE LAMP [[orange]]

= "The Lamp" · [F5a · The Fields Seq I]

INT. JAHN'S LABORATORY — DAYS LATER

Two wires on a stalk, a glass with a solution, a strip of copper. Behind them, on the wires: a LAMP, small, unscrewed from a bicycle headlamp.

Jahn closes the circuit.

The lamp flickers.

And holds.

He sits in front of it and does not move. A tiny light in a back room, fed by a flower. He marvels.


# 5. GREY · TAU, Seq 2 "The Home" (G12–G16)

.INT. CORRIDOR, THE HOME — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Pencil" · [G12 · TAU Seq 2]

Anna walks down the corridor — and stops. In front of her stands a man she did not hear coming.

THE PENCIL (50s, long, thin, grey — the children's nickname explains itself).

He holds out a bundle of paper to her without a word. Old paper, printed on one side, neatly tied.

Anna takes it.

He walks on as if nothing had happened.


.EXT. YARD, THE HOME — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Reckoning" · [G13 · TAU Seq 2]

The MATRON comes striding fast toward the HOUSEPARENT, stomping.

MATRON
Hey!

She holds his list in one hand and waves the houseparent over. In the other: the slip with the 16.

The houseparent understands at once.

The children sense that something is off and gather around the two of them.

She is not loud. She only reads out.

MATRON
It says here: sixteen pieces.
(beat)
There were ten crates.
(she raises the list)
Your list says: one hundred and fifty. Always one hundred and fifty.

The children say nothing. Thirty pairs of eyes do the arithmetic. It is not a hard sum — and it calculates itself backward through the years.

HOUSEPARENT
Fifteen. There were always fifteen in there. Always. Ask — ask anyone you like —
(faster)
They print it wrong. What do they know, over there
— there were spoiled ones in there, I took those out, that's not on there —
Who —

He breaks off.

The silence of thirty children is not a quiet sound.

MATRON
(to the room)
To your places. All of you.

Nobody goes. Not hostile. Just: nobody goes.

The matron looks from the children to the papers in her hand. She is the only one who understands at once what has just come to an end here.

Anna stands nearby. She watches.


.INT. THE LITTLE ONES' DORMITORY — EVENING [[blue]]

= "The Imitation" · [G14 · TAU Seq 2]

FIVE LITTLE ONES (the youngest: 3, 7, 10, 12 and 12) crowded onto one bed.

Anna stands in the aisle between the beds. And does the houseparent:

The walk — arrogant, disdainful gaze. Then the pious face, the upward glance when he lies.

Soundless. Exact. Devastating.

The little ones laugh. First one, then all of them — the smothered, snorting laughter of children who know they could be heard.

Anna does not laugh. She does a second round: the thief sliding a ration into his sleeve while glancing piously upward. The little ones nearly shriek.

At the door, in the dark of the corridor, stands one of the BIG ONES, watching. He does not laugh.


.EXT. YARD, THE HOME — MORNING [[blue]]

= "The Revolt" · [G15 · TAU Seq 2]

At the food distribution a BIG ONE reaches across the table and takes what is not his. The THIEVING HOUSEPARENT holds on to the crate — two seconds, then the boy tears it out of his hands, and the grown-up falls. Too easily. Frighteningly easily.

After that it goes fast: crates changing owners; a door kicked off its hinges; children striking out at houseparents, other houseparents trying to flee. Somewhere something small is burning that nobody puts out.

Fourteen-year-olds are not children you can stop. The old have no strength.

Anna leads nothing. She stands in the smoke at the edge of the yard, the notebook already under her arm, and watches — not triumphant, not frightened. Keeping records.


.EXT. GATE, THE HOME — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Open Gate" · [G16 · TAU Seq 2]

The gate stands open. Nobody guards it anymore.

Anna walks through, without running. The notebook under one arm, the bundle with the half ration under the other.

In the doorway of the side wing — ARCHIVE is written above the door, in old paint — stands the Pencil, watching her go.

He does not raise his voice. He raises nothing at all. He only stands so that she knows he has seen her.

Beyond the gate: the city. Grey, staggered.

She walks in.



# 6. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq II "Two Buyers" (F6–F7a)

.F6 — THE LAUGH IN THE WRONG PLACE [[orange]]

= "The Laugh in the Wrong Place" · [F6 · The Fields Seq II]

INT. HALL — EVENING

A hall, half full: chairs in rows, a podium, a glass of water. At the front a banner, hand-painted. The audience: field workers, craftsmen, a few curious people from the city, hats on their knees.

Jahn is speaking. In public for the first time — you can tell by the hands, which don't yet know where to go. But he speaks well. Too well, by a shade: the sentences are built, every one of them carries.

JAHN
— and I tell you: this plant can do more than smell sweet. It can shine. I have seen it, in a back room, with a lamp off a bicycle. Imagine that lamp a thousand times over. A city that draws its light from its own fields —
(he raises a hand, a little too solemnly)
— a city that would no longer have to belong to anyone. That belongs to itself. Like a man who for the first time stands upright —

Right into it, someone LAUGHS.

A woman, in the fifth row. Loud, short, involuntary — the laugh of someone who has heard an unintended joke that everyone else swallowed reverently.

HELENE, late twenties, an alert mouth. She puts her hand over it. Too late.

Jahn loses the thread. Two seconds of silence in the hall. He looks at her. She looks back, not embarrassed — rather: apologetic, but not sorry.

And Jahn finds the thread again. Differently. Simpler.

JAHN (CONT'D)
I mean: light. For everyone. Out of what grows here.
(Beat)
That's all.

He sits down. The applause is more honest than the applause he would have got for the sentence about the upright man.

LATER, AT THE EXIT. People stream out. Jahn stands at the podium packing up papers he didn't need. Then he sees her, at the door — leaving, coat already half on.

He leaves the papers and goes after her. Not the other way round.

JAHN (CONT'D)
You laughed.

HELENE
You said "upright". In front of a roomful of proud field workers who spend all day bent over.

JAHN
(after a second)
That was — not good.

HELENE
That was funny. The rest was good.

She buttons her coat. He searches for something to say and finds only:

JAHN
Will you come again?

HELENE
If you say something funny again.

She goes. He stays at the door, with the hands that still don't know where to go — and this time he doesn't care.


.F7 — THE TEACHER [[orange]]

= "The Teacher" · [F7 · The Fields Seq II]

INT. CLASSROOM, SCHOOL — DAY

Thirty children, quiet. Not the quiet of fear — the quiet of listening. At the front Helene, sitting on the edge of the desk, no book in her hand.

HELENE
— and the snow lay up to the window, and the father was in the village and wasn't coming back, not tonight. And the boy was alone in the hut. And then he heard it. At the door. From outside.
(she scratches her fingernails along the edge of the desk, slowly)
Scratching. Quiet at first. Then harder. And then something breathed through the gap under the door.
(Beat)
And the boy knew what a wolf is. And he knew: the bolt is old. And he braced his back against the door —

She stops. Thirty faces wait. She lets them wait exactly as long as it can be done.

HELENE (CONT'D)
— and the door held. All night.

An exhalation in the room, thirtyfold.

At the door to the corridor, half in the frame, stands Jahn. He has come with a pretext — a note in his hand, something to do with the movement — and has forgotten the pretext. He listens like the children.

Helene sees him. She doesn't break off.

HELENE (CONT'D)
And behind the door?

A CHILD
(bursting out)
The wolf!

HELENE
No wolf. His father.

The bell rings; the children get up and run out of the room.

HELENE (CONT'D)
White as chalk, and cold.
(she shakes her head, laughing)
We finished on time after all.

The last child runs past Jahn. Helene wipes the board.

JAHN
Interesting story... Maybe a touch too dark?

HELENE
I have to like it too.

JAHN
I tell a thousand people my story. I don't get thirty who hang on it like that.

HELENE
Mine are seven. They listen if there's a door in it.
(she looks at him)
Put a door in.

JAHN
Into a speech about energy?

HELENE
Into everything.

She hangs up the sponge. He still has the note in his hand and still doesn't know what's on it.


.F7a — THE LITTLE LIGHT [[orange]]

= "The Little Light" · [F7a · The Fields Seq II]

INT. JAHN'S LABORATORY — EVENING

The back room. Shelves, glass, the sheets with the curves. On the table, under a cloth: something he doesn't dare announce.

Helene stands in the doorway, coat still on. She is here for the first time; you can tell by the way she looks at everything.

HELENE
So this is it.

JAHN
This is it.

HELENE
I'd imagined more smoke. And an assistant with a hunchback.

JAHN
He's got the day off.

She laughs. He takes the cloth off the apparatus: the stalk in the glass, two wires, a strip of copper. The little lamp from the bicycle headlamp.

HELENE
That's it?

JAHN
That's it.

HELENE
It's smaller than the way you tell it.

JAHN
Everything is smaller than the way I tell it. That's why people come.
(Beat)
Would you like to —?

She would. He shows her where the wires go — her hands, his hands; it takes a moment longer than necessary, and neither of them hurries it.

Then she closes the circuit.

The lamp flickers. And holds.

Helene says nothing. She, who has something to say about everything.

Jahn isn't looking at the lamp. He is looking at her. Then he puts out the light in the room, so that only the lamp burns.

HELENE
(quietly)
And that comes out of the flower.

JAHN
Out of the flower.

HELENE
Why are you showing me this?

JAHN
Because you laugh when I exaggerate.
(Beat)
I wanted to have one thing you wouldn't laugh at.

The little light burns between them.

Then the stalk gives up. The light grows weaker, quite calmly — and goes out.

It is dark. Neither of them turns on a light.

[[Translator's note: in the German, Helene here switches for the first time from the formal "Sie" to the intimate "du".]]

HELENE
(in the dark)
You don't exaggerate at all.

JAHN
Not here.

It stays quiet in the dark room. Neither of them looks for the door.



# 7. GREY · TAU, Seq 3 "The City" (G17–G22)

.EXT. STREETS — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Rules" · [G17 · TAU Seq 3]

The city by day: emptier than a city is allowed to be, and not empty enough to be dead.

Anna walks — and learns.

At a corner: a CHALK X, hip-high, retraced many times. Ahead of Anna a shrouded FIGURE with a bucket turns off without looking at the X. Anna turns off too, following.

A well with a bucket on it, upside down. The figure does not draw water here; it walks two streets further to a well without a bucket. Anna memorizes both.

Doors with signs hanging at them — on the post, at hand height: a strip of cloth, a piece of tin, once a child's shoe.

In front of these doors you walk on the other side of the street. Anna walks on the other side of the street.

Nobody explains anything. Anna asks nothing. We learn the way she learns: by watching.


.EXT. BLANK WALL — DAY [[blue]]

= "Can You Still Smell It?" · [G18 · TAU Seq 3]

A windowless wall, six stories high. On it, huge and faded: an ADVERTISING POSTER.

A glass flacon, perfect of form. A flower, painted like a promise. And a sign, curved like a signature — the Greek letter: τ. Beneath it, in elegant lettering: TAU.

Across it, in tar, in letters that dripped as they were written:

> CAN YOU STILL SMELL IT? <

Anna walks past without reading.


.EXT./INT. OLD DISTILLERY — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Drum Room" · [G19 · TAU Seq 3]

A long brick building behind a courtyard wall, dead chimneys. Above the gate, in old paint, a remnant of the curved sign: τ.

Anna is looking for wire. She climbs in through a window that is no longer one.

Inside: gutted halls. What was wood has been burned; what was metal has been built into other things. The city has eaten this house like all the others.

Anna works her way through the rubble, practiced. At a dead machine she finds what she needs: wire, spring-hard, arm-long. She coils it up.

Then, at the end of a corridor: a door that is still one.

It opens without a sound. In this house, that stands out: somebody oils these hinges.

Behind it: DRUMS.

Two dozen, lined up against the wall, skinned and taut, the heads dull with grease. No dust. Dust lies over this whole city — not here.

Anna stands in the doorway and touches nothing. Her gaze does what her gaze always does: counts, files away. What this is, nobody tells her. Nobody tells us either.

She pulls the door shut, as quietly as you shut doors in other people's houses.

On the way back, across the courtyard: the COURTYARD WALL. Names, written in chalk, one beneath the other — some weathered, some fresh.

Anna walks past without reading.


.EXT. WASTELAND — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Dog" · [G20 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna, motionless behind a remnant of wall, a wire snare in her hand, her eyes on a PIGEON pecking among the rubble.

She throws. Too late —

Something yellow is faster. A DOG, thin, yellow, takes the pigeon mid-leap, lands, stands.

He does not eat. He looks at Anna, the pigeon in his jaws.

Anna looks at the dog. And you can watch her calculate — it is the same face as with the sugar, the thief, the lists: meat for two days. Or that — what he just did — every day.

She crouches down. Waits. The dog comes, in a wide arc, and does not put the pigeon down — but he lets Anna lay her hand on it.

She shares. Half, half. He eats his at once. She wraps hers up.

The dog stays.


.EXT. ALCOVE BETWEEN TWO HOUSES — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "Without Calculation" · [G21 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna sleeps sitting up, her back against the wall. The dog lies behind her, a warm half-circle around her back.

Half asleep, the dog lays his head on her leg.

Anna's hand is in his fur before she notices. For a moment her face is fourteen years old and nothing else — no bookkeeping, no calculation.

She notices. Startles. Pulls her hand away.

The dog lifts his head, looks at her.

She puts her hand back.


.EXT. NICHE BETWEEN TWO HOUSES — MORNING [[blue]]

= "The Morning Below" · [G22 · TAU Seq 3]

The smell wakes her before the light does. The dog is gone.

At the mouth of the niche, one step from her feet: human filth. Fresh. Somebody was here in the night, that close.

Anna gets up, the bundle already in her hand. The disgust is one thing.

The other: she heard nothing. She, who in the home heard every key two doors away, slept through somebody coming within a step of her.

Down here you sleep like a thing left by the road.

Her gaze goes up, along the facades.

The dog comes around the corner, a rat in his jaws.



# 8. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq II "Two Buyers" (F7b–F8a)

.F7b — THE SENTENCE ON THE SQUARE [[orange]]

= "The Sentence on the Square" · [F7b · The Fields Seq II]

EXT. MARKET SQUARE — DAY

A gathering bigger than the hall: the square full, people on steps and walls. At the front Jahn on a crate; the voice carries now.

Beside him, half a step back, stands Lenz. He gives no speech. He stands there, with the hands people know, in the shirt people know — and his standing there is the speech: an educated man preaching common ownership is suspect. A field worker standing next to him makes it true.

JAHN
People ask me: who is this to belong to, when it comes — the light, the works, the whole new life? And I listed it off like the educated man I am: to the city. To the movement. To the cooperative.
(he lets it go quiet)
All correct. All too small.
(Beat)
I walked the fields with a man who knows them. Who smells a row and tells you what the still will do tomorrow. Don't ask him for papers — he has none. He has it in his hands.
(Beat)
And so I say it the way one of you said it, at a kitchen table, and he no longer even knows it was him:
(Beat)
The fields belong to the ones who know them.

The sentence goes across the square like a wind. You can see it arrive: heads lifting; a man repeating it to his neighbour; a woman nodding as if she had always known it.

Lenz, beside Jahn, does not move a muscle. Only his hands, hanging at the seams of his trousers, close once and open again.

Nobody on the square knows whose sentence it is. He himself didn't know until just now that he had said it.


.F8 — THE PROMPTER [[orange]]

= "The Prompter" · [F8 · The Fields Seq II]

INT. KITCHEN, KESSLER HOUSE — NIGHT

The same kitchen, emptier now — only the three of them: Jahn, Lenz, Ruth. Papers on the table, a jug, the remains of the evening.

Jahn rehearses. He stands at the window and speaks a paragraph to no audience, and Lenz sits beside him like a prompter in the box.

JAHN
— and when they ask who is going to pay for it, I say: the same people who have paid for it so far. Only this time they —

LENZ
(cutting in, half aloud)
— get something for it.

JAHN
— this time they receive something for it.

LENZ
Get. "Receive" is the tax office.

JAHN
Get.
(he tries it)
Only this time they get something for it.

Ruth laughs. Not at the line — at the two of them.

RUTH
He says it like he's written it down.

LENZ
He has written it down. I only told it to him.

Jahn starts again — and ruins the line a third time, more tidily than before. Ruth laughs anyway. On principle.

Lenz refills the glasses.

JAHN
(at the window, back in his speaking voice)
And then the ending.

LENZ
You can do the ending.

JAHN
(he says it anyway; for the first time this evening every word sits)
The fields belong to the ones who know them.

For a moment the kitchen is quiet.

On the table, among the papers, lies the same sentence in Jahn's handwriting, underlined. Lenz looks at it briefly. Then he lays a sheet over it.


.F8a — THE LAUGH IN THE RIGHT PLACE [[orange]]

= "The Laugh in the Right Place" · [F8a · The Fields Seq II]

EXT. CHURCH — DAY

The bells. The door opens — and the first thing out is not the bride and groom but the noise coming in: on the square in front of the church stands a crowd nobody invited.

JAHN and HELENE on the top step. She in a light dress, hair done simply; he in the only good suit he owns. For one breath they stand alone. That is the entire private part of this day.

Then the square begins. Cheering, hats, raised arms. Someone shouts his name; then everyone does. It doesn't sound like a wedding. It sounds like the market square.

Blossoms fly — Tau blossoms, whole handfuls, wherever so many came from. They catch in hair, on shoulders, in Helene's bouquet.

The crowd comes up the steps. Hands upon hands. Jahn shakes them the way he has learned: look each one in the face, give each one a second. He has become good at it. He doesn't notice that he is working.

And the crowd pushes itself between the two of them. Half a step at first. Then two. Then Helene is standing at the railing with her bouquet, watching her own wedding like someone who happened to be passing. Beside her, strangers congratulate one another.

From where she stands it looks like this: a man covered in blossoms, shaking hands at his own wedding as if at a rally.

Helene laughs.

Loud, short, involuntary. The same laugh as back then in the hall, fifth row.

And as back then: Jahn hears it. Through the cheering, through the names, through the hands. He lifts his head, finds her at the railing — and lets go of a hand in mid-shake.

He goes through the crowd to her. This time he doesn't wait for the way to clear. He makes it.

JAHN
(reaching her, out of breath)
You laughed.

HELENE
You said "upright". In front of the priest.

JAHN
I did not.

HELENE
No. But you were close.

He laughs — a beat too late, as always.

He takes her hand. The crowd cheers on, about something, about itself. The two of them stand at the railing, blossoms in their hair, and watch it.



# 9. GREY · TAU, Seq 3 "The City" (G23–G29)

.EXT. CHURCH — DAY [[blue]]

= [G23 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna and the dog come to a church. The windows have been smashed in. She goes inside. The dog follows her.


.INT. CHURCH — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Bicycle on the Altar" · [G24 · TAU Seq 3]

The church: high, cold, still. The light falls through smashed-in, blind windows as if through milk.

Anna looks around, fascinated; she has never seen a room like this.

The pews are gone. In the dust, the pale rectangles where they stood — burned, row after row. On the side walls, an almost completely torn-off confessional.

On the altar lies a BICYCLE.

Scrap, eaten hollow like the houses: the spokes harvested, the tyres gone, the chain gone.

On the handlebar dangles the SPEEDOMETER, its cable still attached — a wire, hidden in a sheath.

Wire is wire. Anna takes the speedometer off and sets her knife to it: prying, turning. The housing does not give. Not at the first try. Not at the second.

She pockets it, cable and all.


.EXT./INT. ATTIC ABOVE A DEAD LANE — EVENING [[blue]]

= "Sleeping High" · [G25 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna climbs a facade nobody considers climbable anymore — drainpipe, ledge, window.

The attic: empty, dusty, high. Two ways out — the hatch to the lane, the skylight to the ridge. Anna checks both. Only then does she set down her bundle.

The notebook gets a hiding place: under a floorboard. The food gets another: behind the chimney flue, two rooms away.

In the corner lies something: a SPYGLASS. Small, brass, a dent in the tube — blind. Whoever looks through it sees milk. Deep inside the housing, something loose rattles.

Anna holds it to her eye.

Then she wraps it up and hides it — not with the ration, not with the notebook. A third hiding place. The best one.

She goes to the skylight, takes off her shoes and considers the right one in the moonlight. The sole is slowly coming loose. Then she sets it aside, carefully.

She lies down and looks into the lane below. Whoever sleeps high hears the city from above.

The dog has found the staircase she did not take. He lies down at the door as if that were his post.


.EXT. WASH HOUSE — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Archive of Not-Finding" · [G26 · TAU Seq 3]

A low building at the end of a lane, and it does something no other house in this city does: it breathes. Steam from every crack, from blind windows, out from under the door.

People with bundles go inside. Anna stands on the other side of the street and watches. Beside her, the dog. She takes him into a side lane and hides him there behind a few barrels. With her hand she tells him to stay, and puts down a piece of bread for him. Then she goes to the wash house.

Baskets are stacked by the door. Beside the door a RACK, roughly built, full of shoes — pair beside pair, of everyone inside. Nobody guards it.

Anna takes off her shoes and puts them with the rest. Her hand stays a moment on the right one, the one with the loose sole. Then she lets go.

She picks up a basket and goes in, barefoot.


.INT. WASH HOUSE — DAY [[blue]]

= [G27 · TAU Seq 3]

A single room, and the room is steam. Tubs, washboards, benches, gutters in the floor. At the far end of the room a low door, firelight behind it.

The door opens, and TWO MEN come in, between them a VAT on two poles, rags around the handles. The water steams. Behind them, for a moment: the FIRE ROOM — a blackened kettle, and beneath it the only fire in this city allowed to burn by day. It eats what the houses give up: chair legs, floorboards, a length of banister.

Hot water, in this city, exists exactly here. Nowhere else.

The men go along the tubs, topping up — one measure each.

The GROWN-UPS OF THE CITY, for the first time many at once. Many shrouded, long-sleeved, grey — the MARKED; they wash what a sleeve will yield: hands, face, the neck down to the collar. Others have stripped: bare arms, bare backs, skin in the steam. What rule lies behind it, you cannot see. You see only this: nobody looks.

And you see the order of the house, which nobody explains. Those who can wash themselves. The weak are washed — an OLD WOMAN who can no longer lift her arms, a MAN as thin as his stick. VOLUNTEERS wash them. Nobody assigns them, they come of their own accord. The hot water belongs to the weak first; nobody explains that either.

Anna sets the basket down. And watches.

The steam veils and unveils, in waves: arms, wrists, the strip of skin between glove and sleeve.

Anna organizes herself a rag. A bowl. The next time the vat comes past, she dips the bowl in like everyone else. It takes no two minutes, then she is kneeling among the volunteers, washing what is pushed her way — a hand, an arm, a back. A child that works is asked no questions here.

The old woman holds out her forearm to her like a piece of wood. On the inside: a MARK. Anna washes over it, once, twice. It does not come off. She looks at it — exactly as long as a washerwoman would look. No longer.

She does not look at faces. Only at forearms. One after the other, tub by tub.


.EXT./INT. WASH HOUSE — EVENING [[blue]]

= [G28 · TAU Seq 3]

The dog lifts his head. Anna comes out of the wash house. He runs to her and walks along at her side.


.EXT./INT. ATTIC ABOVE A DEAD LANE — NIGHT [[blue]]

= [G29 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna sits before a small fire that gives light; in front of her lies the dog. On a spit, a skinned rat.

Anna draws marks on paper from memory, looks at them and compares them in her head, and crosses them out, one after another.

She lays the notebook aside.

Anna looks over at the dog; he looks up at her.

ANNA
Nothing again.

She takes the rat and cuts it up with an old knife, throws a piece to the dog, and bites into her own share.



# 10. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq II "Two Buyers" (F9–F9b)

.F9 — TWO BUYERS [[orange]]

= "Two Buyers" · [F9 · The Fields Seq II]

INT. THE MAGNATE'S HOUSE, RECEPTION ROOM — DAY

A room in which everything is old and everything is real: wood with centuries in it, carpets that swallow footsteps. No gold, anywhere. This wealth has nothing to prove.

On a low table, on silk: a FLACON. Tau. Set down as if by chance. Nothing here is by chance.

JAHN sits on the edge of an armchair you are meant to sink into. City shoes that were too loud on this carpet.

Of the MAGNATE we see the hands first: they pour tea, calm, precise.

MAGNATE
I was told you don't drink tea with me.

JAHN
I'm here.

MAGNATE
That's what I said too.

He hands over the cup. Jahn takes it and sets it down without drinking.

MAGNATE (CONT'D)
You know, I know people well. My father had a steward, a gifted man. One year he promised a double harvest — new method, everything calculated, the numbers were even right. The city cheered him. People celebrated in advance.
(Beat)
It turned out a good harvest. The best in years — just not a double one. So they put his windows in. A man who had given them the best harvest in years. Not for what was missing, Mr Jahn. For the hope.
(he sets down his cup)
Half the city is reciting your sentence. I'll be brief, you're a busy man.
(Beat)
I'm not offering you money. You would refuse, and we would both have lost an evening. I'm offering you an institute, here on the campus. You do your research in peace, come back with sound results. With instruments this country doesn't have. Your name over the door.
(he smiles)
And one day, perhaps, my chair beside yours. Not the other way round.

JAHN
Thank you, I do appreciate the offer, but I have to decline. We have the studies, a plan. Out here — I wouldn't know what there was to do.

The magnate waits. Listening costs him nothing.

JAHN (CONT'D)
A hundred kilos.
(he nods at the flacon)
A hundred kilos of harvest for that.

MAGNATE
(grinning)
Two hundred.

For a moment Jahn is thrown.

JAHN (CONT'D)
Out of the same — two hundred kilos I make light for a street. For a whole winter. Your fields can do both. Surely you can see that.

MAGNATE
Oh, I see it.

He takes the flacon off the silk. Holds it to the light — with two fingers, like something alive.

MAGNATE (CONT'D)
Have you any idea what people are willing to pay for this little bottle?

He puts it back.

MAGNATE (CONT'D)
The three hundred kilos are not a mistake, Mr Jahn. The three hundred kilos are the price. You are, I'm afraid, confusing added value with yield.
You are offering to make the rarest thing in the world into the most ordinary. I decline.

Jahn stands up.

JAHN
The fields can make light. Sooner or later someone will do it. With you or without you.

MAGNATE
(kindly)
Without me, nobody builds here. You know that.

Jahn says nothing. It is the only answer that is true.

He goes. The flacon stays on the silk — untouched, you can see it on the table.

The magnate is left alone with two full cups. Then he takes the flacon and folds the silk back around it, corner by corner, carefully — the way you wrap something that still has a journey ahead of it.

He smiles as he does it. It is not a wicked smile. That is the worst thing about it.


.F9a — THE GIFT [[orange]]

= "The Gift" · [F9a · The Fields Seq II]

INT. HELENE'S FLAT — DAY

A room with books, a window onto the street, a table where exercise books get corrected.

A knock at the door. Helene gets up and opens it.

HELENE
Hello?

MESSENGER
A parcel for you.

HELENE
(puzzled)
For me?

Helene looks at the address. It says Helene Jahn.

HELENE (CONT'D)
Thank you.

She closes the door, sets the parcel on the table and considers it.

No sender. No card.

Helene opens it. Paper. Inside, a fine box without lettering. Under that, silk, folded corner by corner.

Inside: the flacon. Tau. The rarest thing in the world, in her hand, smaller than a thumb.

She looks at it for a long time. She isn't quite sure where it could have come from. Jahn?

She takes a small BOX from the shelf — tin, with a pattern worn smooth — lays the flacon back into the silk and the silk into the box, and the box right at the back of the shelf, behind the books. The fine box goes in the bin.

Then she sits back down to the exercise books. It takes a while before she sees the first mistake again.


.F9b — THE THINGS FROM BEFORE [[orange]]

= "The Things from Before" · [F9b · The Fields Seq II]

INT. HELENE'S FLAT — DAY

The room with the books — but the books are already in crates. Pale patches on the wall where pictures hung. On the table where the correcting was done, a last stack of exercise books.

Jahn carries crates. He does it earnestly and a little wrong, like everything practical: too many books in one, too few in the next.

JAHN
(lifts, sets down again)
What's in there?

HELENE
The dictionaries.

JAHN
All of them?

HELENE
You wanted to carry.

He carries. In the stairwell you can hear him talking to someone who is congratulating him. Somebody congratulates him everywhere now.

Helene alone in the room. She clears the shelf, row by row —

and then there stands the TIN BOX. Right at the back, the worn pattern.

She pauses.

She doesn't open it. She glances at the door — Jahn's steps below, voices — then she lays the box in the open crate, right at the bottom, the books on top, the exercise books on top of those.

When Jahn comes back she is just shutting the crate.

JAHN
And that one?

HELENE
From before. Nothing important.

He nods and carries it out.

LATER. The room is empty. Helene stands in it a moment longer. Light falls through the window onto the table where nobody corrects anything any more.

She takes the key from the hook and pulls the door shut behind her.



# 11. GREY · TAU, Seq 3 "The City" (G30–G34)

.EXT. GANG TERRITORY / MARKET — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Toll" · [G30 · TAU Seq 3]

A square that was once a market and is one again — except that all the traders are fourteen. Stalls made of door panels, tolls at the entrances, rituals nobody explains.

Anna wants through. Two GANG BOYS step into her path, casually, the way a door swings shut.

She gives way to the left — there is a third. To the right: a stall, a wall. It looks like chance and is craft: three steps, and Anna stands in a corner from which the only way on is through the boys.

The dog beside her. Still, for now.

THE LEADER comes without hurry: fifteen, wiry. He appraises Anna. The bundle. The notebook under her arm.

LEADER
What's that?

ANNA
Paper.

He holds out his hand. Anna gives him the notebook.

The leader leafs through it. Crossed-out signs, page after page. He glances at Anna — a look like a shrug: mad, but worthless. He hands it back.

Then he nods to the boys. One reaches for Anna's bundle.

Under Anna's jacket, at the waistband: the old knife. Her hand lies on it without our having seen it go there.

She calculates — faster than you can breathe; we only see it because we know this face by now: three in front of her, one beside, a wall at her back.

The hand comes out from under the jacket empty.

And then there is something YELLOW between Anna and the boys.

The dog. Low, the fur on his back a crest, teeth bared — and out of the thin body comes a sound nobody expected of him. The foremost boy snatches his hand back. The dog snaps — air, close. A warning, not a wound.

The boys give way. All but one.

The leader has stayed where he is. He looks at the dog.

LEADER
He hunts.

It is not a question. Anna does not answer. That is answer enough.

LEADER
The dog, then.

Anna looks at the dog.

The dog still stands between her and the boys. Ready. He is waiting for a sign from her — and whatever it says, he will do it.

Anna calculates.

She raises her hand. Flat, calm, downward: stay.

The dog stays. A gang boy lays the rope over him, carefully, with respect for the teeth.

At the edge of the square, a boy who sits bent over a trap has lifted his head. The spring between his fingers stands still.

From the boy's point of view: Anna does not turn around. She walks through the entrance they now clear for her and grows smaller between the stalls.

The boy watches her go.

At one of the stalls she stops: crockery, harvested from dead houses — pots, spoons, a TIN CUP with a dented rim.

Anna unwraps her bread, breaks off half and lays it on the door-leaf. The TRADER (fourteen, like everyone here) looks at the bread, then at her. Then he takes it.

The cup disappears into Anna's bundle.


.EXT. EDGE OF THE TERRITORY — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Mended Sole" · [G31 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna passes the stall of the boy who just watched her go. She looks at him:

A BIG BOY making himself small, bent over a trap.

The boy glances up at her briefly; their eyes meet. Caught, he turns away and busies himself with the trap again — a bracket, a spring, a mechanism he tenses and releases with two fingers, tenses and releases.

As she passes, he looks at her shoes.

JAKOB. But we do not know that yet.


.EXT. WELL WITHOUT A BUCKET — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Well" · [G32 · TAU Seq 3]

The good well, two streets past the one with the upturned bucket.

Four of the MARKED stand in line, with cans and jugs, at the distance this city keeps between everyone.

Anna joins the end of the line, her tin cup in her hand.

Then the line moves. Not forward — apart.

The last one steps aside. The woman before him too. One after another, wordless, without looking at Anna: a lane of waiting people, and at its end the well.

Anna stands still. You can watch her looking for the price. She finds none.

At the winch her gaze catches: the handle new, the rope freshly spliced — wire work, neat as seams. Two seconds, three.

She draws water, fills her cup and holds the well bucket out to the first in the line. He nods and takes it. She goes and drinks. Behind her the line closes again, as if nothing had happened.


.EXT. WASH HOUSE — DAY [[blue]]

= [G33 · TAU Seq 3]

The breathing house. The baskets by the door.

Anna puts her shoes on the rack — without looking, with the grip of a habit — picks up a basket and goes in.

The steam takes her, as the house takes everyone.


.INT. ATTIC — EVENING [[blue]]

= [G34 · TAU Seq 3]

Anna comes "home" and sits down. She takes off her shoes. Stops.

The torn sole of the right one: mended. Properly, with wire and a piece of rubber.

Anna looks at the sole for a long time. She does not know when it happened. Neither do we.

Her gaze goes to the door. To the empty spot where the dog used to lie.

For the first time since the home she looks as if something had escaped her.



# 12. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq III "The Works" (F10–F13a)

.F10 — DEADLINES LIKE HOLIDAYS [[orange]]

= "Deadlines Like Holidays" · [F10 · The Fields Seq III]

EXT. MARKET SQUARE — DAY

The same square, but the crate has given way to a stage: boards, a railing, flags. On a board, large, a date. Beneath it: THE WORKS — FOUNDATION STONE.

A SPEAKER for the city, whom people know and don't like, addresses the crowd. Beside him sits Jahn, whom people know and do like, and he is not allowed to speak — he has spoken already, yesterday, the day before.

SPEAKER
— and who would have thought that of all people our greatest son — yes, I'll say it: our greatest son — would not have to build his works alone? That the city stands behind him, its citizens, its businesses —

Applause, steered. At the edge of the square: wagons bringing timber. Already.

SPEAKER (CONT'D)
— and that the most generous hand in this city has opened without our having to ask? Ladies and gentlemen: we are building. Not in five years. Not in three. We are building now.

Applause, honest this time.

SPEAKER (CONT'D)
Faster, friends. The future doesn't wait.

Jahn applauds along. You can't see anything in his face. Only that he starts half a second later than everyone else.


.F10a — TWO PLANS [[orange]]

= "Two Plans" · [F10a · The Fields Seq III]

INT. OFFICE OF THE MOVEMENT — DAY

A room full of paper. On the wall a PLAN of the works — bigger than Jahn ever drew it; his own design hangs next to it, smaller, in pencil. On the table newspapers: THE FUTURE DOESN'T WAIT. A photo of the magnate, smiling, beside a photo of Jahn, not smiling.

Jahn alone. He lays the two plans on top of each other, against the window. The big one swallows the small one whole.

A STAFFER, young, in the doorframe:

STAFFER
They're asking whether the date for the topping-out is fixed. The press wants —

JAHN
Which date?

STAFFER
The one on the board.

Jahn looks at the plan. Calculates. Says nothing. Then:

JAHN
Tell them it's fixed.

The staffer leaves. Jahn stays at the window, with two plans of which only one still counts.


.F11 — THE ACCIDENT [[orange]]

= "The Accident" · [F11 · The Fields Seq III]

INT. LABORATORY — DAY

No institute. A converted storeroom: shelves, glass, two small kettles, on the wall a row of ATOMISERS. On a shelf, in chalk: TAU. Beneath it stalks, bundled, sorted by day of harvest.

On the wall a board: curves, data, tidy. All the curves flat.

THE ASSISTANT (twenties, quick, talks while he works) sets a new batch in the small kettle: pure Tau harvest. JAHN takes notes at the table. Two OTHERS weigh out.

ASSISTANT
Third row's done. If it keeps up like this we'll be finished before the rain, and then —

He opens a valve. A fine mist stands in the backlight.

It smells good. Then it smells too good. Then sweet — and somehow wrong.

ASSISTANT (CONT'D)
— then tomorrow we can do the fourth —

He drops. Mid-word, no knees, no grasping at air: like a light switched off.

For a second it is completely still. The mist stands.

Then JAHN wrenches the window open. One word, more breath than voice:

JAHN
Out.

The others grab the assistant by the legs, by the jacket. The door stays open.


.F11a — THE BREAK [[orange]]

= "The Break" · [F11a · The Fields Seq III]

EXT. YARD OUTSIDE THE LABORATORY — DAY

The assistant lies in the yard, three faces above him. Long seconds.

Then he opens his eyes. And finishes the sentence:

ASSISTANT
— manage it.

He looks into the faces above him. He doesn't understand them.

ASSISTANT (CONT'D)
What?

For him no time is missing.

One of the others laughs, thinly. It stops by itself.

ASSISTANT (CONT'D)
(sitting up)
What happened?

JAHN
You took a break.

It is built as a joke, and it works as a joke. The assistant grins crookedly. The others too.

Only Jahn goes back to the laboratory door and looks at the row of atomisers. Behind them, on the wall, the board with the curves. All flat.

He says nothing.

LATER. The assistant is the last to go back to work. Outside the door he stops for half a step — takes a breath. And carries it in with him.


.F12 — THE REPORT [[orange]]

= "The Report" · [F12 · The Fields Seq III]

INT. LABORATORY — EVENING

The window is still open. The others have gone. The assistant is tidying up; Jahn sits at the table, the lab book in front of him, open at today.

The line for the afternoon is still empty.

ASSISTANT
Shall I put it in? I've got the time, roughly —

JAHN
I'll do it.

He writes. The assistant looks over his shoulder — and reads, slowly, what is there:

ASSISTANT
"Batch seven, valve opened too far by mistake. Work interrupted. Ventilation improved."
(Beat)
That wasn't an interruption.

JAHN
It was a break.

He says it kindly. Exactly as kindly as in the afternoon.

ASSISTANT
Mr Jahn —

JAHN
If this is in the paper tomorrow — "The traitor knocks his own people flat with the stuff he wants to hang into the city's grid" — then they build the works anyway. Only without us. Without the one man who knows how strong the stuff is.
(he closes the book)
We know now that we have to ventilate. We ventilate. Nobody needs to know more than that.

The assistant says nothing. He looks at Jahn the way you look at someone you believe and don't quite.

ASSISTANT
And the curves?

JAHN
We measure again. With pure harvest. From tomorrow.

The assistant nods. Takes his jacket and goes.

Jahn alone. He opens the book once more. Reads the line. Lets it stand.


.F13 — THE BOILER WILL HOLD [[orange]]

= "The Boiler Will Hold" · [F13 · The Fields Seq III]

INT. JAHN'S STUDY — NIGHT

A desk, a lamp, a window behind which the works are growing — you can see the cranes as black strokes against a lighter sky.

On the desk two papers. On the left, a few weeks old, with pencil notes in the margin: the first report. You can read the heading if the camera is close enough: TOO SOON. TOO BIG. On the right, fresh, in ink: the second.

Jahn is writing the second. We read along, in excerpts:

"...the effect of the vapours in open air is considerably stronger than assumed in the previous series (incident of the 14th, see appendix). Cause: pure early-hour harvest, where the studies were based on mixed daily harvests..."

He pauses. Writes on:

"...from this follows a second consequence. With pure harvest the pressure in the boiler rises above the values determined for mixtures..."

He looks up. To the window. To the cranes.

Then he gets up, goes to the shelf, pulls out a file: PRESSURE VESSEL — INSPECTION. Leafs through it. A value, underlined, with a safety margin that was once generous.

He calculates in the margin. Crosses out. Calculates again. The number that remains is not bad. It is just no longer generous.

He sits down. Writes the last paragraph:

"...the boiler is designed for an overpressure that covers the values to be expected. A modification is no longer possible at the current stage of construction without delaying completion by several months. The design will ..."

He falters for a moment.

"hold."

He reads the last paragraph through once more. The pen wavers, hesitant, over the paper.

He signs.

Then he puts the first report in the drawer — not in the wastepaper basket, in the drawer — and closes it.

The second he takes with him.


.F13a — FACE DOWN [[orange]]

= "Face Down" · [F13a · The Fields Seq III]

INT. JAHN AND HELENE'S FLAT — LATER THAT NIGHT

Helene is asleep. On the bedside table beside her: a stack of exercise books, corrected. Jahn comes in quietly, lays the report on his table by the window, face down.

He looks over at her. She doesn't wake. She won't read it — he won't give it to her, and she won't ask; she has other books to mark.

He puts out the light.



# 13. GREY · TAU, Seq 4 "The Boy" (G35–G38)

.EXT. GANG TERRITORY, WORKSHOP CORNER — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Mender" · [G35 · TAU Seq 4]

Anna comes back. Her sole has been mended, and she does not know what it cost — open accounts she can bear less well than hunger. The pretext: a rusty padlock without a key, which she lays on the plank in front of Jakob.

He takes it without asking where from, what for.

Anna waits for the question. For any question. None comes. She sits down on a barrel that stands by his stall.

While he works, we see his place in the tribe:

A gang boy brings a cart with a broken axle and leaves it without a word. The leader collects his trap, tensioned, oiled. He inspects it, tests it, makes a half turn and gives Jakob a respectful nod.

Jakob is the tolerated one, the one who makes things run. The only one, his whole bearing says, who was taken in here without a beating.

He wears a jacket that almost fits him. Almost — the way things almost fit that you grow into.

The lock springs open. He hands it to her, with the new key made of wire.

Anna lays a half pigeon on the plank, wrapped — the toll you lay for work in this city.

Jakob looks at the little bundle. Then he pushes it back, with one finger, and goes on working.


.EXT./INT. THE HOUSE WITH THE CHILD'S SHOE — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Second Shoe" · [G36 · TAU Seq 4]

The door with the CHILD'S SHOE on the post. We know it. You walk on the other side of the street.

Anna stands on the other side of the street and watches. For a long time, the way she watches: on the chimney a bird's nest. The windows blind for years. No path in the rubble before the door.

She crosses the street. And goes in.

INSIDE: dust like snow. A living room nobody moved out of — it simply came to an end.

Anna works quietly and fast, with the hands of the practiced: a candle stub. A piece of rubber. Matches, swollen, perhaps still good. What burns and what mends. Everything else she leaves.

Then the second door.

A CHILD'S ROOM. A bed too small for anyone this city still has.

Anna studies the door frame: PENCIL MARKS, one above the other, each year a little higher. She traces them with her fingers.

They stop.

Anna stands in the doorway and takes: nothing.

She pulls the door shut the way you shut doors behind which someone is sleeping.

OUTSIDE: Anna steps into the street. In passing, with a movement that looks like nothing, she straightens the shoe on the post that was hanging crooked.

Then the street is empty. The shoe hangs straight.


.EXT. LANES / WASTELAND — DAY [[blue]]

= "Crates from the Sky" · [G37 · TAU Seq 4]

A beating in the air, still far off.

The city reacts before we understand: doors, footsteps, running — children in front, out of every lane in the same direction, the marked behind them, as fast as their legs will give. For the first time, the whole city runs.

Anna runs with them.

THE WASTELAND. The HELICOPTER: high, too high for faces. It circles once, drops — padded crates, tumbling — and turns away before the first one hits. No wave, no sign. Over there throws the way you feed where you do not want to touch.

The gang is there first — of course it is there first. But nobody grabs anything for himself. The boys open the crates, count, stack; the scramble becomes a distribution, the running becomes a line.

The LEADER stands on a remnant of wall and conducts with his chin: those over there, open that one, you wait. At the market his gang takes toll. Not here. What the sky throws belongs to everyone first.

One crate bursts on impact. TINS roll, the little ones chase them — one of them catches a tin and lets it vanish into his jacket.

LEADER
(without raising his voice)
Back.

The boy puts the tin on the stack. Nobody looks at him for it.

The marked arrive last — and do not leave empty-handed: things have been set aside for them, without anyone having said so.

Anna too receives her share, counted out, wordless — from the same boys who took her dog at the toll.


.EXT. GANG TERRITORY, WORKSHOP CORNER — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Speedometer" · [G38 · TAU Seq 4]

Anna comes. Jakob is waiting.

In front of him a plank across two barrels, tools on it, and beside them, wrapped in a cloth: the speedometer from the church — the thing Anna could not get the wire out of.

Jakob unwraps it. Round, glass, a needle at zero. He hands it over with both hands, solemnly, like a medal.

JAKOB
Fixed.

Anna turns the thing over. No crack, no rust. And no bicycle, anywhere — there are no bicycles left in this city.

ANNA
It doesn't show anything.

JAKOB
(gravely)
Zero. Exactly right.

Anna looks at him for a long time.

Then she stands differently — chin up, a half turn, paying him respect: the GANG LEADER, to the millimeter. Soundless. Exact.

Jakob's laughter comes too late. Then it comes entirely — the whole body, a laughter that falls over. He is embarrassed at once, right in the middle of it, and keeps laughing.

Anna smiles a little. She pockets the speedometer.

She leaves. Jakob looks after her. Anna looks back and, smiling, shakes her head.

JAKOB (calling)
What's your name?

She lifts the speedometer into the air.

ANNA
Anna!

Jakob looks eased. Then he sorts tools that are sorted.



# 14. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq III "The Works" (F14)

.F14 — A WORD, NO MEASURE [[orange]]

= "A Word, No Measure" · [F14 · The Fields Seq III]

INT. THE MAGNATE'S HOUSE, STUDY — DAY

Not the reception room. A smaller one, without silk: a desk, a window, a man at work. We see him from behind — the immaculate back, the hands closing a folder.

A VISITOR stands in front of the desk. We see him only half, a coat, a hat in his hand. No face we need.

VISITOR
— in the laboratory. Two weeks ago. One of his people fell over. They didn't report it.

MAGNATE
Fell over.

VISITOR
Keeled over. During a test. He got up again. They say they ventilate more now.

A pause. The hands set the folder aside.

MAGNATE
Anything else?

VISITOR
Nothing else. They're measuring again.

MAGNATE
(after a while)
A man who keels over and gets up again.

It sounds almost amused.

MAGNATE (CONT'D)
Thank you. That's all.

The visitor leaves. The magnate sits still for a moment. Then he pulls over another folder: CONSTRUCTION SCHEDULE. Leafs to the topping-out. Takes a pen.

And does not move the date back. He moves it forward. A week.

A man who keels over and gets up again — that is the size of damage he is reckoning with. A stuttering works, an embarrassed movement, a politician who got his sums wrong. No more.



# 15. GREY · TAU, Seq 4 "The Boy" (G39–G42)

.EXT. GANG TERRITORY, WORKSHOP CORNER — LATE AFTERNOON [[blue]]

= "The Half-Done Thing" · [G39 · TAU Seq 4]

The workshop corner. On the plank, half taken apart: the SPYGLASS — Anna's spyglass, at last a real defect. Lenses, rings, the empty tube: parts in a row only Jakob can read.

Jakob works. Anna sits on the second barrel, in a waiting position that has long stopped being one.

Then Jakob looks up — not at Anna: at the light. The stand of the sun above the roofs.

And packs up. Wordless, hurried, with an order that knows no haste and is haste anyway: parts into the cloth, cloth under his arm. He takes everything with him.

ANNA
Where are you going?

JAKOB
Have to go.

No invitation. No look back.

The boy has a clock in his body.

Anna stays seated for a moment, considering. Then she follows him.


.EXT. LANES — LATE AFTERNOON [[blue]]

= "To the Door" · [G40 · TAU Seq 4]

Through lanes she does not know. Jakob walks fast and naturally; Anna keeps her distance like an investigator.

A quarter, narrower, lower. Washing lines without washing. The door signs hang closer together here — a strip of cloth, a piece of tin, one every few houses.

Then a door. Jakob goes in without closing it, and climbs the stairs.

Anna stops. At the threshold. Then she follows him up.


.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — LATE AFTERNOON [[blue]]

= "Ruth" · [G41 · TAU Seq 4]

From the door, with Anna: a room that is workshop and home at once. Tools on the wall, tidy. A bed by the window.

In the bed lies a WOMAN. RUTH (50s, built broad and made narrow by the illness; a face that has laughed a lot, and you can still see it).

Jakob already sits at her bed, a bowl of water beside him, washing her arms.

The arms are covered in marks. Many, old and new. Jakob washes them with the casualness of a boy who has never known anything else.

Anna, in the doorway, watches.

Ruth turns her head. Sees Anna. Seconds.

RUTH
In or out. But don't stand there in the light — I don't have much of that left.

She steps across the threshold.

Ruth watches Jakob wring out the cloth.

RUTH
He washes me every day. I tell him: boy, save yourself the trouble, the worms will take me dirty too.
(pause)
But no. Jakob wants it done properly.
(to Anna, confiding)
He doesn't get that from me.

Anna laughs. Short, through the nose, against her will — we do not know this laugh yet, it is as new as the room.

Jakob gets up, puts the bowl away and goes outside.

OUTSIDE, at the door: Jakob breathes. Once, twice, three times. Deep. Then he goes back in.


.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Cot" · [G42 · TAU Seq 4]

The spyglass, half reassembled, on the workbench. It has grown late; the kerosene burns low.

Nobody sends Anna away.

She sits in the corner by the door. Ruth sleeps. Jakob tidies tools that are tidy.

In the other corner: a COT. Stretched flat, unused. A bed on which a man should be lying who is not there. Beside it, on the floor: a folded blanket, a pillow on top — Jakob's place, a hand's breadth from the empty bed. Anna sees it. Catalogues it. Does not ask.

LATER: Anna, half asleep, on a blanket on the floor.

Next door, behind the thin wall: sounds. An awl through leather. Thread being pulled through. Even, practiced.

Anna falls asleep before she can decide what it is.

IN THE MORNING: The cot is as smooth as in the evening. On the workbench lies the spyglass. Finished. Nobody is there who could have been the one.



# 16. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq III "The Works" (F15–F15a)

.F15 — THE TOPPING-OUT [[orange]]

= "The Topping-Out" · [F15 · The Fields Seq III]

EXT. CONSTRUCTION SITE OF THE WORKS — DAY

The shell, vast against the sky: concrete, steel, the skeleton of a hall in whose middle something round is growing — the boiler, still open, like an eye without a lid.

Topping-out. A wreath on the highest girder, flags, a band playing too loud. The drums we know already. Workers with beer mugs. The movement with banners. The city, watching.

Above the main gate LETTERS are being mounted, each three metres high, one after the other, on ropes:

J — A — H — N

Then a gap. Then: W — O — R — K — S.

JAHN WORKS.

Jahn stands in front of it. Beside him Lenz. They watch the last letter click into place.

Lenz lays his hand on one of the girders — not like a man testing steel; the way he would lay his hand on a plant, to feel whether it is healthy.

LENZ
Grown big.

JAHN
Bigger than it should have.

LENZ
Everything grows bigger than it should. Anton too.

JAHN
(smiles)
How old is he now?

LENZ
Two. He can already say "no". Nothing else yet, but that one well.

They both laugh, briefly. It is their last conversation before the catastrophe, and it is small talk. The friendship does not break. It is missed.

The band starts up again. Someone calls for Jahn — the press, a photo. He goes. Lenz takes his hand off the girder, slowly, and wipes it on his trousers.


.F15a — THE PHOTO [[orange]]

= "The Photo" · [F15a · The Fields Seq III]

EXT. CONSTRUCTION SITE OF THE WORKS, IN FRONT OF THE SHELL — DAY

A PHOTOGRAPHER for the movement, with a camera on a tripod, is arranging people. The shell in the background, seen from below, proud.

PHOTOGRAPHER
Four. Four will do. With the flags. — You, Mr Jahn, in the middle. Mr Kessler next to you.

Lenz comes, reluctantly, with a flag someone has pressed into his hand.

PHOTOGRAPHER (CONT'D)
And the ladies. Mrs Kessler — with the little one, yes, with the little one, that's good. And —

He searches. His eye falls on Helene, standing at the edge, without a flag.

PHOTOGRAPHER (CONT'D)
You. Please. Far left.

Helene steps into the picture. Takes a flag. Stands the way you stand when you don't like being photographed, turned a little — and one hand lies on her dress, low, the way hands lie when they are guarding something.

Ruth, with Anton on her arm, laughs at something Lenz has said. The photographer raises his hand.

PHOTOGRAPHER (CONT'D)
Hold it.

One breath. Then it is a picture.

It will hang in the city, in classrooms and offices.

PHOTOGRAPHER (CONT'D)
Thank you. That's it.

The four dissolve. Ruth hands Lenz the child. Helene lets the flag fall as if she had never held it.



# 17. GREY · TAU, Seq 4 "The Boy" (G43–G44)

.EXT. MARKET — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Line of Sight" · [G43 · TAU Seq 4]

The gangs' market, everyday life: trading, counting, tolls.

Somewhere in between: two GANG BOYS on the ground, a tangle of sleeves and dust. Then one tears free and runs — something in his fist. What, you cannot see.

The other picks himself up and shouts after him:

GANG BOY
Go to Jahn!

Nobody on the square turns their head.

Anna and Jakob at a stall — she is trading her half pigeon for potatoes and eggs, he is waiting.

At the other end of the square: movement. A MAN (gaunt, long sleeves), drunk — the slow, concentrated walk of a drunk who knows that everyone knows what he is. He crosses the market.

Jakob sees him first. Long before Anna could see him.

And then, casually, as if by chance: Jakob changes sides, takes half a step to the left, turns to Anna, shows her something at the stall — onions, a sieve, whatever.

He places himself exactly in her line of sight. He stays in it, with small corrections, until the man is off the square.

Anna has seen nothing.


.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Laugh" · [G44 · TAU Seq 4]

The flat has grown used to Anna. She sits in her place by the door; Jakob is looking for a tool he tidied away himself.

RUTH
The pair of you, honestly. One tidies up, the other guards my door.
(pause)
And keep the conversation down — I need my sleep.

Anna laughs. Through the nose, against her will.

And Ruth goes still.

She looks at Anna — too long, and suddenly quite clear and quite far away at once: the way you look at something that last stood in a room half a lifetime ago.

RUTH
Anna, let me look at you... It feels good, having you here. Finally there's laughter in this place again. I've missed having one like you.

ANNA
It used to be funnier here?

RUTH
Much. ...Long time ago.

They look at each other and smile.



# 18. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq III "The Works" (F15b–F17)

.F15b — COUNT ALONG [[orange]]

= "Count Along" · [F15b · The Fields Seq III]

EXT. PATH TO THE LOOKOUT — LATE AFTERNOON

A path that turns in hairpins up a hill. A railing, freshly painted. Two people climbing — she ahead, he behind, a little out of breath because she is faster.

HELENE
Count along.

JAHN
What?

HELENE
The steps. You count the steps here. My father did it, and his before him. Whoever reaches the top and knows the number, the place belongs to them. For the day.

JAHN
And if I lose count?

HELENE
Then it belongs to me. As always.


.F16 — THE PLACE [[orange]]

= "The Place" · [F16 · The Fields Seq III]

EXT. THE LOOKOUT — LATE AFTERNOON

The top. A railing, a patch of meadow, a bench somebody knocked together long ago.

And the view.

The whole city at once: the roofs, the river, the fields to the horizon — in bloom, a sea of colour. And in it, by the river, new, vast, the crane still beside it: the works. Almost finished. Above the gate the letters, too small to read from here, but you know what they say.

Helene goes to the railing. Jahn beside her.

HELENE
This is my place. Since I was six. I've never shown it to anyone who wasn't family.

JAHN
And now?

HELENE
Now it's yours too. A little.
(Beat)
If you know the number.

JAHN
Two hundred and fourteen.

HELENE
Two hundred and twelve. You stumbled twice.

She looks out over the city.

HELENE (CONT'D)
We're a bit early.

She leans over to him and whispers it into his ear — not spoken aloud, only into the ear. He hears it. We don't.

Then, without taking her eyes off the city, quite calm:

HELENE (CONT'D)
I'm having a child.

Jahn turns to her. She still isn't looking at him. She is looking down at the city, at the fields, at the works by the river.

HELENE (CONT'D)
I wanted to tell you here. It's the only place that's beautiful enough.

Jahn takes her hand. He says something — we don't hear it, it is lost in the wind that always blows up here. She laughs. She lays her head on his shoulder.

And over her shoulder he looks at the works. Almost finished. Above the gate, his name.

She sees the happiness. He sees it too. And he sees what lies beneath the happiness — he alone, because only he knows the number that is no longer generous.

He says nothing. He holds her hand tighter.

Below, the sun goes down behind the works, and its golden light settles over the city.


.F17 — FIELD WOMEN [[orange]]

= "Field Women" · [F17 · The Fields Seq III]

INT. KITCHEN, KESSLER HOUSE — EVENING

The kitchen, emptier than it used to be. The table for six, and two sitting at it: Ruth and Helene. The jug is there, but nobody drinks much. Anton is asleep next door.

The men are out. They are often out now.

RUTH
Show me.

HELENE
There's nothing to see yet.

RUTH
I'll see it anyway. Show me.

Helene stands up, a little embarrassed. Ruth lays her hand on her belly, flat, with the matter-of-factness of a woman who has done this before.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Nothing. There's nothing there yet.
(Beat)
And still I saw it on you when you came in. You educated ones look different when you're pregnant. As if you'd read it up in a book.

Helene laughs.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Does anyone else know?

HELENE
Paul.

RUTH
Anyone else?

HELENE
Nobody else. I wanted to — I don't know. Keep it to myself first.

RUTH
Then I'm the first to know. After him.
(she sits down)
I'll remember that.

She takes a swallow. Then, casually, as if it were nothing:

RUTH (CONT'D)
Me too.

HELENE
You too — what?

RUTH
(she lays her hand on her own belly)
Two months. Lenz has known since yesterday. Anton doesn't — he'll scream.
(she laughs her laugh)
Two at once. The kitchen's going to be too small.

HELENE
It's always been too small.

RUTH
That's why it's good.

They clink, jug against glass.

RUTH
My grandmother used to say: a man builds you a house or a child. Never both at once — or one of them comes out crooked.
(she raises the jug towards the city, where the building is going on)
Now our two are building the biggest works in the country. And here we sit —
(the free hand on her own belly)
— both of us. So you work out what —

Helene is already laughing. Ruth tells the ending anyway, to herself, and then laughs loudest of all.

RUTH (CONT'D)
In spring they'll both be here. Then we'll have a party. Here.

HELENE
Here?

RUTH
Where else. One table, both children on it —

HELENE
On the table?

RUTH
To show them off. And the kitchen full like it used to be. And the men have to sing.

HELENE
Can Lenz sing?

RUTH
No.
(Beat)
Exactly.



# 19. GREY · TAU, Seq 4 "The Boy" / Seq 5 "The City's Lie" (G45–G48)

.EXT. ROOF ABOVE THE LANE — EVENING [[blue]]

= "Two Secrets, One Peace" · [G45 · TAU Seq 4]

Two children, a flat roof, remnants of food between them.

Anna's notebook lies beside her — the binding has come loose, the endpaper hangs.

Jakob sees it. Holds out his hand.

Anna looks at the hand. One second. Two.

Then she gives him the notebook — closed.

Jakob sews the binding. Awl, thread, the sounds from that night. He does not open the notebook while he works. Not even halfway. He works on the closed book, more awkwardly than he would have to.

Anna watches him. Closely. The whole time.

He hands it back. The binding holds.

JAKOB
The thread is better than the notebook.

ANNA
I know.


.INT. — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Night, Second Time" · [G46 · TAU Seq 4]

Again the floorboards. Again the yellow circle.

THE NIGHT, A SECOND TIME. But:

The lamp stands on the table, not on the floor. The curtain hangs at a different window. And the whispering woman's voice says words we know:

MOTHER (O.S.)
(whispering)
... a place, high above everything ...

A KNOCK. The bolt. The loud word that does not come through.

The arm stretches into the light: the gun, the mark — hyper-sharp, exactly as the first time. Only everything else has moved.

Whoever was paying attention now mistrusts their own memory for the first time: that is not how it was.

Or is it?

Anna starts out of sleep. Attic, darkness, her breath.

Her lips begin to count.


.INT. CORRIDORS OF THE DYING ROOMS — DAY [[blue]]

= "Carrying Water" · [G47 · TAU Seq 5]

A long corridor in a house that was once something else. Doors behind which people are dying. VOLUNTEERS carry water — buckets, jugs, cups.

Anna carries water.

We know this already. It has become her everyday life: another house, the same work, the same gaze. She holds cups, supports arms, sees wrists.

And casually, in the passing of the days: whoever brings water eats along. A DYING WOMAN wordlessly pushes half a plate toward her; an OLD MAN lays bread on the rim of her bucket without looking at her. The dying share what they no longer need.


.INT. A DYING ROOM — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Old Woman with the Beat, I" · [G48 · TAU Seq 5]

A bed by the window. THE OLD WOMAN (80s, small, tough). She drinks Anna's water in small sips. Anna lays her notebook on the windowsill. On a leaflet that slips out you can see a headline, "15 Years of Terror," yellowed. The old woman looks at it and taps her fingers on it.

@THE OLD WOMAN
Is that from today?

ANNA
What? That — no, probably not.

@THE OLD WOMAN
Well? What do you think?

ANNA
Don't know.


The old woman looks at her. Then she laughs — a short, hard laugh that delivers the beat along with it.

@THE OLD WOMAN
Terrorists don't build power plants, child. Nothing could ever be fast enough.
(she taps the beat on the edge of the bed)
Faster. Faster. Faster.

She hands Anna back the glass.

@THE OLD WOMAN
Jahn never convinced me, back then. But intent?

Anna takes the glass. Registers it.



# 20. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq IV "The Night of the Works" (F18–F21)

.F18 — THE HALL [[orange]]

= "The Hall" · [F18 · The Fields Seq IV]

INT. HALL — NIGHT

The same hall as at the first speech — empty, only the floor and the stage. Stacked against the wall: CHAIRS. Four hundred. You can't see it all at once, but you can guess it.

Lenz is setting them out. Row upon row, with a string as a guide so the rows come out straight. A second HELPER has given up; he sits on the edge of the stage and drinks.

HELPER
It's four hundred. It's past midnight. Nobody's going to count the rows tomorrow.

LENZ
I'll count them.

HELPER
You count everything.

LENZ
Someone has to.
(he shifts a chair, a finger's width)
The fellow can't drive a nail in straight. He holds a hammer like a fountain pen.
(he laughs)
But he stood on my field for three hours and kept his mouth shut till I was done. Nobody else can do that. So I do the nails. And the chairs.

HELPER
And him?

LENZ
He talks.
(he sights along the row, one eye shut)
And tomorrow he talks, and the chairs stand straight, and nobody notices. Good. It's him they're meant to notice, not me.

He sets the next one. The helper shakes his head and gets up after all and helps.


.F18a — THE OPEN WINDOW [[orange]]

= "The Open Window" · [F18a · The Fields Seq IV]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, BEDROOM — NIGHT

Ruth sleeps, heavily, on her side, her belly in front of her. Beside her, in his little bed pushed up against the big one: Anton, two years old, one arm over his head, the way children sleep who fear nothing.

The window stands open a crack. It is warm for the time of year.

You can hear, very far off, the river.


.F19 — THE LIGHT OVER THE RIVER [[orange]]

= "The Light Over the River" · [F19 · The Fields Seq IV]

INT. HALL — NIGHT

Lenz sets the last chair. Four hundred. He steps back, looks at the rows — straight, all of them. He is satisfied the way you are satisfied with a thing nobody will see.

He takes his jacket.

Then the night sky loses its colour.

Not loud. At first only this: through the tall windows it grows light, wrongly light, a light that doesn't come from the street. A trembling in the panes. Then, late, the sound — a blow so deep you feel it in the floor before you hear it.

Lenz stands with his jacket in his hand.

Then he runs.


.F19a — AGAINST THE STREAM [[orange]]

= "Against the Stream" · [F19a · The Fields Seq IV]

EXT. STREETS — NIGHT

Out of the hall, into the street. Above the roofs, towards the river: a light that won't go out. A glow that colours the sky from below.

People. First a few — windows open, doors. Then more. Then the street is running. All in one direction: away from the river.

Lenz runs the other way.

Against the stream, past shoulders, against faces. Someone grabs his arm — "Not that way!" — he tears free. Street after street.

Over everything, already now: a smell. Sweet. The smell of the fields, but wrong, too much of it, as if someone had set a whole year's harvest alight at once.


.F19b — THE YARD [[orange]]

= "The Yard" · [F19b · The Fields Seq IV]

EXT. KESSLER HOUSE, YARD — NIGHT

His house. The roof is standing. The windows are not.

In the yard: Ruth. On her knees, alive, in her nightdress, with glass in her hair and Anton in her arms — lifeless. She is screaming. It is not a word. It is only a sound, always the same one.

Lenz comes through the gate.

He sees her. Recoils.

Then he goes to Ruth and kneels down in front of her, takes both of them in his arms, and she goes on screaming, into his shoulder. He holds her, and above them the sky stands in a wrong light and smells of flowers.

LATER. Dawn is breaking. The glow by the river has grown smaller, not gone out.

Lenz sits outside in the yard. Ruth sits leaning against him. Neither has closed an eye tonight. In front of them, wrapped in cloth, lies Anton.


.F20 — THE COMMUNIQUÉ [[orange]]

= "The Communiqué" · [F20 · The Fields Seq IV]

INT. RADIO STUDIO — MORNING

A small room, a microphone, a man with headphones. In front of him a sheet, freshly typed, with a STAMP that has only just dried.

The man at the microphone clears his throat. Reads.

Meanwhile: we see a desk in another room, hands laying a sheet into a folder. Hands we know.

ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
Attention, an announcement from the city administration. Last night an explosion occurred at the power plant on the river. According to initial findings it was a terrorist attack from the circle around the politician Jahn, who had the works built under his name.


.F20a — THE RADIOS [[orange]]

= "The Radios" · [F20a · The Fields Seq IV]

EXT./INT. CITY — MORNING

Radios. In kitchens, in shops, on a windowsill in a yard where people stand with blankets around their shoulders. The voice runs everywhere at once.

ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
According to the authorities, the politician Jahn died while fleeing. The population is asked to avoid the vicinity of the works and to follow the instructions of the security forces. Further announcements will follow.


.F20b — RADIO OFF [[orange]]

= "Radio Off" · [F20b · The Fields Seq IV]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, KITCHEN — MORNING

The radio on the windowsill. Lenz stands in front of it, alone, and switches it off.


.F21 — BACKYARDS [[orange]]

= "Backyards" · [F21 · The Fields Seq IV]

EXT. BACKYARDS — NIGHT

The same night, earlier. While it is still burning.

A backyard, then a second, a wall, a passage. Two figures in coats, without light: a MAN who goes ahead and keeps turning round. A WOMAN who does not slow down; one hand she holds on her belly, under the coat, where nothing shows yet.

They stop at a corner. Above the roofs the glow. From a window somewhere, the radio: "— from the circle around the politician Jahn —"

The man looks at the woman. The woman looks at the man.

HELENE
(whispering)
They're saying you're dead.

JAHN
(whispering)
Then that's what I am.

It is the last time Paul Jahn could have spoken aloud. He did not.

Here the story changes sides. From now on it belongs to the one who stayed.



# 21. GREY · TAU, Seq 5 "The City's Lie" (G49–G54)

.EXT. RUIN BY THE RIVER — EVENING [[blue]]

= "JAH ERK" · [G49 · TAU Seq 5]

The works. Huge, burst open, gnawed at by plants — the ruin of a pride.

Above the main gate, letters three meters tall, half fallen:

> JAH   ERK <

What is missing lies below in the rubble and has been burned, built in, vanished, letter by letter.

Anna walks past along the bank of the river in front of it.


.EXT. THE FIELDS — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Harvest" · [G50 · TAU Seq 5]

Beyond the last house: the FIELDS. Man-high stalks as far as the moonlight reaches, a blossoming that looks grey in the dark — and a smell that numbs; you can see it on the faces.

The gang moves out, a dozen children with knives and sacks. Anna goes along; her place is at the back, Jakob beside her.

LEADER
(half aloud, walking)
Cut, don't tear.

Cutting is done: umbels, in a rhythm the smallest ones master fastest. Pressed into bales, tied.

At the BARRIER — concrete, wire, a dead floodlight — a section of the barrier is opened from the outside. A hole. The bales change sides without a word. From the other side come chocolate, schnapps, kerosene and cigarettes.


On one of the crates, absurdly clean in all the mud: a LABEL with a curved sign: τ. We know it. From the poster.

Anna cuts, presses, carries. Her share: one bar of chocolate.


.EXT. EDGE OF THE FIELDS — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Flacon" · [G51 · TAU Seq 5]

On the way back, where the field turns to mud: something gleams.

Anna bends down. A FLACON — the glass from the poster, gone dull, the beautiful stopper tarnished. Inside: a remnant, a few drops, darker than water.

She looks around. Nobody. She opens it.

Her knees give way. She sits down in the mud, right in it. Her eyes run over — without anything being sad. Her face knows nothing, only the tears run.

She holds the flacon in both hands like a chick.

That is how Jakob finds her. He is trailing the others a little, as always. He stops. Looks at her, in the mud, with the vial.

He does not ask.

He crouches down next to her, within reach, no closer. After a while, dead serious:

JAKOB
If you're going to eat them — the flowers taste of soap.

Anna laughs. And cries. Both at once, and she cannot explain either.

The stopper goes back on the flacon. The flacon disappears into her jacket, where the notebook lives.

NO INSERT on the vial. No memory. Whoever carries the hideout from the first night may remember. Anna cannot.


.EXT. ROOF — EVENING [[blue]]

= "The First Run" · [G52 · TAU Seq 5]

Over the city comes a sound like a second heart: DRUMS. Not fast. Deep.

Anna is up first — of course she is up first. Jakob follows, grip by grip, grey in the face. He hates heights. He comes anyway. Anna sees it.

From up here, across the street canyon:

The MAIN STREET. Torches, a procession. DRUMMERS at the front, then — two dozen RUNNERS, old men and women, in light clothes. With BARE ARMS.

For the first time in this film, someone displays marks like medals.

Anna, at the edge of the roof. From her jacket: the spyglass.

For the first time through whole lenses — and the first thing she sees sharp is bare arms in torchlight.

The city stands along the edges of the street, dense, still — and sings. Something half-forgotten, more hum than song — drawn out, heavy, in repetitions, like something that must have come into being at work. It does not sound sad. It does not sound glad either. It sounds old.

At the edge of the city, where the street opens into the fields, the runners begin to run.

Really to run. Like children. Like the mad.

The stalks swallow them one after another. The singing stops, not all at once — it frays. Then only the blossoming is there, and the drumming, which slows like a dying heart. Then that stops too.


.EXT. BACK ACROSS THE ROOFS — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "Only More Honest" · [G53 · TAU Seq 5]

Two children on a ridge, one behind the other, slow. For a long time nothing but their steps.

Then, without occasion:

JAKOB
The old people say it's like a holiday. Only more honest.

He says it like a joke you have inherited and do not quite understand.

Anna does not answer.


.EXT. DISTILLERY COURTYARD — MORNING [[blue]]

= "The Table the Morning After" · [G54 · TAU Seq 5]

The morning after the run. Anna has followed the path of the procession — backwards, to where it began — we know the house from before.

The courtyard: kettles, cold, stacked into one another. Wrung-out cloths hang over the wall. Something happened here yesterday that we did not see.

In the middle of the courtyard: a TABLE. On it, in good order: the runners' things. Jackets, folded. A comb. A knife with a horn handle. A pair of boots, soles side by side.

Nobody guards it.

Anna takes her place in the gateway and watches.

Over the course of the morning they come, singly, with time in between: one of the MARKED takes a jacket, holds it against herself for a moment, takes it along. A GANG BOY — at the market he would be loud — takes the knife and is quiet about it. An OLD MAN takes the boots and puts his own in their place, the worse ones.

Nobody takes two things.

Towards noon the table is almost empty. The comb is still there.

Anna has watched the whole time. She leaves without having taken anything.



# 22. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq V "Four Years" (F22–F24)

.F22 — THE MONTHS [[orange]]

= "The Months" · [F22 · The Fields Seq V]

EXT./INT. CITY — VARIOUS DAYS

The city dies in months. It isn't told. It is shown:

— A market where the stalls grow fewer. Where one is missing, a strip of cloth hangs on the door behind it.

— A man at the well pushing up his sleeve — and on the inside of the arm there is a mark, dark, small. He looks at it. Pulls the sleeve back down. Long sleeves from now on.

— A truck with soldiers unrolling wire. Concrete blocks set down on the street. A sign, fresh: RESTRICTED AREA. NO ENTRY. The truck drives away, outward. It doesn't come back.

— The fields. Uncut. The stalks stand and bloom as if nothing had happened, shoulder-high, to the horizon. Nobody harvests any more. The smell still lies over everything — and now people know what it is.

— The distillery: the gate shut, the τ above the gate already paler.


.F22a — THE NEW CHILD [[orange]]

= "The New Child" · [F22a · The Fields Seq V]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, BEDROOM — DAY

A bed too many: the little one, up against the big one. Nobody has cleared it away.

And beside it, in a basket, new: an infant. JAKOB. He cries the way infants cry, for no reason and with every right.

Ruth lifts him up. She has grown thinner since the night — not much, but you can see it. And on her forearm, as the sleeve slips: a mark. One. Small.

She doesn't look at it. She looks at the child.

Lenz stands in the doorway. He has a bag in his hand — from somewhere, bartered, begged. He sees the three things in the room: the woman, the new child, the empty bed.

There are houses in which a bed stands too many. Into this one a child is born.


.F23 — THE QUIET DRINKER [[orange]]

= "The Quiet Drinker" · [F23 · The Fields Seq V]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, KITCHEN — NIGHT

The kitchen that was once too small for twelve. Now one man sits in it.

On the stove an apparatus Lenz has built from copper pipe and a pot — a small still, crooked, but it runs. It drips into a glass. He holds the glass up to the lamp. Clear. He smells it — once, one draw through the nose, like at the mash back then.

Then he drinks.

It is the same man who once distilled Tau, with the same nose, at the same copper. Only what comes out is rotgut. The same skill, fallen.


.F23a — THE MARKS [[orange]]

= "The Marks" · [F23a · The Fields Seq V]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, BEDROOM — NIGHT

Ruth is asleep. On her arm, lying over the blanket: two marks now. Lenz sits beside the bed, the glass in his hand, and looks at the arm. Smells — involuntarily, he can't switch it off — and reads what the smell says.

He drinks.

He sets the glass down. Lays the blanket over Ruth's arm. Sits.


.F24 — THREE ROOMS [[orange]]

= "Three Rooms" · [F24 · The Fields Seq V]

INT. HIDEOUT — DAY

Three rooms. We never see them whole — always only the section: a corner with a table, a window with a curtain that never opens all the way, a door with a bolt on the inside.

The man who once spoke to a thousand people sits at the table and whispers. He whispers everything now. Professionally.

The woman is dressing to go out: coat, scarf, long sleeves — like everyone outside. Hardly anyone knew her face; she can go. He can't.

HELENE
(whispering)
Bread, if there is any. And the woman at the well says a delivery is coming.

JAHN
(whispering)
Be careful.

HELENE
I'm always careful. That's my profession now.

She smiles. She kisses him. She goes — the door, the bolt from outside, her steps on the stairs.

He stays seated.

From the next room: a child waking up. A small crying, then a calling — not a word, not yet.

He gets up and goes to it, and we hear his voice, very softly, saying something soothing.

It is ANNA. She is one year old and knows the world as what falls through the gap in a curtain.



# 23. GREY · TAU, Seq 6 "The Good Days" (G56)

.EXT. WATER TOWER — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Missing Step" · [G56 · TAU Seq 6]

Anna climbs the water tower — routine, grip by grip, the highest point of the quarter. At the top, from her jacket: the spyglass.

She looks out over the city.

Suddenly a HILL, at the far end, behind an old depot. A path turning upward in switchbacks, a railing as a thin line — and at one point, where the path disappears behind the sheds, it breaks off. A gap in the picture.

Like a staircase with one step missing.

Anna looks. Looks away. Looks again.



# 24. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq V "Four Years" (F26)

.F26 — THE FAMILY WORD [[orange]]

= "The Family Word" · [F26 · The Fields Seq V]

INT. HIDEOUT, SLEEPING CORNER — EVENING

A mattress on the floor, a blanket, the kerosene lamp turned down low. Anna lies with her head in her mother's lap, a little wooden horse in her arms. Tired. Helene sits with her back against the wall.


HELENE
(whispering)
Anna, little Anna. You know. Up there, high up above the city, there is a place. You take the path that turns. Along the railing. You count the steps.

ANNA
Mama, I know.

HELENE is moved. A tear runs down her cheek.

HELENE
And in the evening..

HELENE bends over Anna and whispers. Strokes her hair and kisses the top of her head.

ANNA
(whispering)
When are we going?

HELENE
When you're big. The three of us. And you count.

ANNA
I can count to twenty.

HELENE
That'll do for a start. Sleep.

Anna closes her eyes. The little horse slips out of her hand. Helene lays it down beside her, against the wall, where it belongs.

In the other room, by the door, sits the father; that's where he sits in the evenings — whoever sits by the door hears the stairs first. You can hear him, barely, turning a page. That too is one of the sounds of this family.



# 25. GREY · TAU, Seq 6 "The Good Days" (G57–G60)

.EXT. PATH TO THE LOOKOUT — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Ascent" · [G57 · TAU Seq 6]

The path in switchbacks, the railing: rusty, gone in places.

Anna walks ahead — and her lips are counting. Soundless, out of habit, out of something older than habit. She does not notice it herself.

Jakob is along too, at the back. Pale. Carefully he walks behind her.


.EXT. LOOKOUT — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Panorama" · [G58 · TAU Seq 6]

At the top.

The wide view: the whole city at once. The ruin with its fallen letters. The fields, a grey sea to the horizon. The barrier: a thin line. And beyond it, incomprehensibly —

world. Simply going on.

Jakob arrives, his eyes opening wide. He tries to breathe the view in. He comes to stand next to Anna. We see both faces.

For the first time Anna stands at a high place and is not alone. Jakob, with his fear of heights, all the more so.

Anna takes his hand.


.EXT. PATH DOWN FROM THE LOOKOUT — DAY [[blue]]

= "Gone as in Gone" · [G59 · TAU Seq 6]

The way back, in switchbacks. Anna ahead, Jakob behind, one hand on the railing where there is one.

ANNA
Has anyone ever gone through, back there?

JAKOB
(without stopping)
One of the gang — his uncle knew someone who tried.

ANNA
And?

Jakob shrugs. It is not a shrug. It is the entire answer.

JAKOB
Gone.

ANNA
Gone as in through? Or gone as in gone?

JAKOB
Gone as in never seen again.


.EXT./INT. OLD CINEMA — LATE AFTERNOON [[blue]]

= "The Cinema" · [G60 · TAU Seq 6]

Anna and Jakob walking, a route like any other: lanes, dead shops, facades with their lids down.

Then Jakob stops.

Above an entrance Anna would not have looked at: a CANOPY on thin struts. Beneath it, behind blind glass, a LETTER BOARD — black grooves, push-in letters. Some letters have fallen or been taken; what stands, stands with gaps — the way the ruin by the river carries its name.

The programme from back then. Three lines, three titles, half readable. Nobody ever took them down. The same week has been playing for fifteen years.

JAKOB
Come.

And he is already inside.

INSIDE, THE HALL: high, dark, cool. Jakob's steps know the way.

Light falls through a burst skylight. The floor: empty. No chairs, no benches — only SCREW HOLES in the screed, in rows, dozens of them, like a dictation without words.

The seat rows have been burned.

At the front: the SCREEN — what is left of it. A remnant, cut away at a slant; cloth is precious. The frame still shows how big it was.

Anna stands among the screw holes and looks around. She catalogues — and arrives at nothing.

ANNA
What was this?

JAKOB
They threw light at the wall here. Stories.
(pause)
Everyone looked in the same direction. There were chairs here, in rows.

Anna looks at the remnant of the screen. Then across the screw holes, the rows. She tries to imagine people, that many, all with the same gaze. You can see that she does not manage it.

JAKOB (CONT'D)
Come on, it gets even better.

THE PROJECTION BOOTH: a steep stair, a cubicle with a view of the hall. In it, under a tarpaulin: the PROJECTOR.

Jakob lifts the tarpaulin off. Metal, lenses, a crank. His reflex reaches out ahead of him: he opens a flap, turns, checks. The machine is heavy and whole. It could be saved.

Jakob's eyes search the booth, the shelves. Empty tin canisters, stacked, open.

JAKOB
(dry; the speedometer tone)
It works. There is just nothing left to put in.

Anna, at the booth door:

ANNA
I have no idea what you're talking about.

JAKOB
Must have been great, back then.


They go. At the door Anna turns around once more.

Through the open door the afternoon falls into the hall — a bright, slanted RECTANGLE on what is left of the screen.

It is the only picture this cinema still shows. It runs as long as the door stands open.



# 26. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq V "Four Years" / Seq VI "The Envelope" (F26a–F28)

.F26a — THE DROP [[orange]]

= "The Drop" · [F26a · The Fields Seq V]

INT. HIDEOUT — DAY

A grey day. In the back room the father is sleeping — he keeps half the night by the door; the morning he sleeps through. These are the hours when the flat belongs to the two of them.

Helene is tidying — what you tidy in three rooms: a crate, a bundle, things that migrate from one place to another.

Anna on her arm, because she wants to be carried, although she has long been able to walk.

Right at the bottom of the crate, under the things from before: the TIN BOX.

She listens for a moment towards the back room: the breathing of sleep, even. Then she sets Anna down on the crate, opens the box. Silk. The FLACON.

ANNA
What's that?

HELENE
That's —
— that's from before. It smells nice. Do you want to smell it?

She opens it — very carefully, the way you open something there is only one of — dabs a single drop on the back of her hand. She tests it first. Then she holds her hand in front of the little one's face.

Anna smells.

Her face: first nothing. Then something that has no word. She smells again. She lays her own small hand on her mother's, as if a smell could be held onto.

ANNA
That smells nice. Again?

HELENE
No. That's enough. Otherwise it's gone.

She closes the flacon. The silk. The box. Puts it back in the crate, right at the bottom.

But she keeps the hand near Anna a while longer, and Anna has her nose against it, and neither of them says anything.


.F27 — THE LINE [[orange]]

= "The Line" · [F27 · The Fields Seq V]

INT. HIDEOUT — EVENING

Helene comes back the way she always comes back: the bolt, the coat, the bundle. But she doesn't put the bundle down. She stands in the doorway.

Jahn looks up.

HELENE
(whispering)
I saw Ruth. At the well. She didn't recognise me — she hardly recognises anyone any more, they say.

JAHN
And?

HELENE
She had a little one on her arm. A boy, a year old maybe.
(Beat)
And Anton —

She doesn't need to go on. He knows it before she says it. Perhaps he has always known it, since the night.

HELENE (CONT'D)
That night. In the house. He was asleep.

Jahn sits at the table for a long time.

Then he pulls a sheet of paper towards him. And a pen. And begins — a line, any line, a sign that he is alive, that he knows, that he —

He writes three words. We don't read them.

He looks at the three words.

And then he looks at the door. At the curtain. At the room where a child is sleeping who would not sleep any more if this line took the wrong way. A sheet of paper, a messenger, one word too many at a well — and the version that declares him dead would suddenly have an address.

He puts the pen down.

He takes the sheet and holds it into the lamp until nothing of it is left.

Helene says nothing. She understands it.


.F28 — THE WOMAN WHO GOES SHOPPING [[orange]]

= "The Woman Who Goes Shopping" · [F28 · The Fields Seq VI]

EXT. WELL / ALLEYS — DAY

A woman goes shopping. That is all you see: a woman in a coat, with a scarf, with long sleeves, among women in coats with scarves and long sleeves. She queues. She barters. She goes.

For four years she has done this right: never the same time, never the same route, never one word too many. She is the most inconspicuous of the inconspicuous.

We follow her from behind.

A few people come towards her. Among them a man. He looks past her — at first. Then he stops, we in front of him, Helene walking on. He sorts his thoughts.

Turns round and follows her.



# 27. GREY · TAU, Seq 6 "The Good Days" (G61–G66)

.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — MORNING [[blue]]

= "Ruth's Good Day, I: The Condensed Milk" · [G61 · TAU Seq 6]

Ruth's good day. She is half sitting up, there is light in the room, and she is talking.

RUTH
Ten years now they've been throwing crates out of the sky at us. You know what I've kept, out of that whole sky?
(pause)
The condensed milk.

Jakob grins over his work. Anna, at the table, laughs her nose-laugh.


.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — MIDDAY [[blue]]

= "Ruth's Good Day, II: Anton" · [G62 · TAU Seq 6]

The light has wandered. Ruth is lying down again. Her gaze finds Jakob, and something in it has slipped.

RUTH
Anton. The window.

Silence.

He closes the window. He holds her hand until she sleeps.

Anna watches.


.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — EVENING [[blue]]

= "Ruth's Good Day, III: Jakob" · [G63 · TAU Seq 6]

The light is almost gone. Ruth wakes. She looks at Jakob — quite clear, clearer than all day.

RUTH
Jakob.

Once. Right. She reaches for his hand.

Jakob holds still, mid-movement.

She says no more. She falls asleep again.


.EXT. WORKSHOP YARD — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "Lenz Drinks Nothing" · [G64 · TAU Seq 6]

The window of the workshop flat: lit, small. Inside, Jakob sits at the bed.

In the yard stands the man from the market.

LENZ (late 50s, gaunt, long sleeves; hands that were once good at something). Jakob's father.

Beside him, on the ledge of the wall: a bottle. Schnapps, full, the cork in.

Lenz stands and drinks nothing.

LATER. The light in the window has grown small.

Jakob sits at the bed. He rises and stands at the bed for a long time, without moving.

Then he opens the window. Wide.

Lenz looks at the open window. He understands it the way you understand something you have waited for all night.

He sits down on the ledge, next to the full bottle. He leaves it closed.

That is how the two of them stay, each on his side of the night: the boy at the open window, the man beside the full bottle. Until it grows light.

A quiet death.


.EXT. WASTELAND / BURIAL GROUND — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Small Funeral" · [G65 · TAU Seq 6]

A piece of wasteland the city has turned into something like this — rows of flat stones, wooden markers without names.

There are no coffins; wood is warmth. The dead are sewn into cloths, the flat stones from the ruins lie against the dogs. No names stand here.

Ruth's cloth is neatly sewn. The seam is fine.

Few people. Jakob. Lenz, two steps beside him — two steps that nobody closes. The leader has come, with two of the gang; they stand at the back holding their caps in their hands, and you can see: they have done this too often for their age. Behind them, the cart from scene 29, "The Mender."

Words are spoken, few. We do not hear them. We do not need to.

Anna stands at the edge, where you stand when you belong to nobody.


.EXT. CITY — DAY [[blue]]

= "How the Home Fell" · [G66 · TAU Seq 6]

After the grave. They walk

For a long time, nothing.

Then, into the walking, without a run-up:

ANNA
At the home I knew things. Where the sugar was. Who crossed the yard at night.

Jakob does not look at her. That is his way of listening.

ANNA
Everyone wanted sugar. I handed it out. For half a loaf.

JAKOB
And if they'd caught you?

ANNA
Not me.

Jakob nods.

ANNA
One of the houseparents stole. From the rations, for years. Everyone knew.
(pause)
Once, there hung a slip of paper from over there on one. With the number of rations.

JAKOB
And?

ANNA
Left it at the administration.

JAKOB
And then?

ANNA
Waited.

Jakob almost stops walking. Then he laughs — the laughter that falls over, in the middle of the street, on the day of a funeral. He is embarrassed at once and keeps laughing.

JAKOB
Waited how. Until someone reads it?

ANNA
Was safer.

JAKOB
(still laughing)
And the houseparent?

ANNA
Then the home was over.

Jakob looks at her from the side — long. For the first time since the grave his face is not grey anymore.



# 28. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq VI "The Envelope" (F29)

.F29 — A RIGHT TO KNOW [[orange]]

= "A Right to Know" · [F29 · The Fields Seq VI]

INT. THE MAGNATE'S HOUSE, STUDY — DAY

The small room, without silk. The hands on the desk. In front of them an ENVELOPE, open, and beside it what is going in:

A slip of paper with an address — a street, a house, a floor. And a second paper, several sheets, a copy, neatly typed. You can read the heading if you want: REPORT. And further down a sentence that is underlined: "...the design will hold."

The hands lay the sheets into the envelope. First the address. Then the report.

You see no face. You hear a voice, tempered, saying one sentence — to someone standing in the room whom we don't see:

MAGNATE (O.S.)
I feel the man has a right to know.

That is all. No order. No name.

The envelope changes hands. The hands remain behind, empty, clean.




# 29. GREY · TAU, Seq 6 "The Good Days" (G67–G68)

.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Prompter" · [G67 · TAU Seq 6]

The room is the same and is not: the bed Ruth lay in is made. The tools on the wall, tidy. The cot empty.

LENZ sits at the table. In front of him stands water he does not touch. He is sober.

ANNA sits on the stool opposite — which she has turned, without thinking, toward the door. Jakob is out getting food.

LENZ
The fields. Four in the morning, in summer. That's when the smell was best — before the sun, while the dew still holds it down. We ran, to be out there by four. Ran. To work.

Anna turns toward him.
Lenz looks at her. For a long time. Almost a smile.

LENZ
I had a friend who couldn't tell a joke. Cleverest man I ever —
(breaks off, starts again)
He could explain a plant to you till you wept, it was that beautiful. But a joke, three sentences — he couldn't carry one across the street in one piece. He always gave the punch line first. Always. Worried you might miss it.

Anna listens the way she listens to everyone. Her eyes go once, casually, to his sleeves. Buttoned.

ANNA
(casually; the casualness of an investigator)
What was his name?

A breath. Lenz's thumb rubs along the edge of the table.

LENZ
My friend.

Anna registers it.
And Lenz talks on as if nothing had been asked:

LENZ (CONT'D)
Ruth always thought him a bit stiff — she liked his girl better. (smiling to himself) The two of them always had plenty to laugh about....
I prompted him. For years. I stood next to him like at the theatre. Sometimes I fed him his own jokes, half aloud, and people laughed, and he took the bow.
(pause)
That was my best one. That nobody noticed who was telling it.

Anna says nothing. But she does not get up.

LENZ (CONT'D)
This one he never got across the street in one piece. Watch.

He straightens up. Barely. But he straightens up.

LENZ (CONT'D)
A man asks the drunkard: why do you drink? Says the drunkard: to forget. Asks the other: forget what?

Lenz lets the pause stand. Exactly as long as it must.

LENZ (CONT'D)
Can't remember.
(beat)
So it's working.

Anna laughs. Once, short, through the nose. Against her will.

Lenz looks up as if a light had come on. He goes to the window, sees nothing.

LENZ
(quietly)
Another?


.EXT. STREET / X-STREET — DAY [[blue]]

= "Stay" · [G68 · TAU Seq 6]

Anna, alone, on one of her rounds.

On the other side of the street, at a trot: the DOG.

Anna stops. He does not. He has not seen her.

She follows him. Not like someone calling — like someone watching: at a distance, along the house walls.

The dog slips through a half-collapsed archway — and is gone.

At the corner: a CHALK X, hip-high, retraced many times.

Anna stands before the X. The dog is beyond it.

Chalk does not apply to dogs. The pigeons nest where nobody goes — the X-streets are the best hunting ground in the city, and it belongs to no one, because no one enters it.

Anna looks up the street, down the street. Nobody.

Then she walks past the X.

THE X-STREET: quiet in a way the city does not otherwise know. The facades are standing. Behind them stands nothing — through the windows you see sky. What was wood has been burned; what was metal has been built into other things. The houses have been eaten hollow: faces with nothing left behind them to carry.

Everywhere: PIGEONS. On the ledges, in the window holes, under the eaves. The only place in the city where something lives in abundance.

The dog is on a BALCONY on the first floor — up a slope of rubble. Under the eaves: nests.

Then he sees Anna.

He goes still. Completely still. Ears forward.

Anna comes closer, below, in the street. Slowly. She wants to say something — and has nothing. She never gave him a name. There is no word to call him with.

Her hand rises. Halfway. Flat.

The dog stays.

Anna slowly climbs the same slope of rubble.

At first it is only a trickle. Plaster, a fine curtain of dust. The dog looks at Anna, Anna looks at the dog.

Then the balcony tears out of the wall.

No sound from him. Only the house: a breaking, a blow, dust taking half the street. The ground shakes. Once.

Then the dust stands in the air, and nothing else.

Anna digs. With her hands, without a sound, stone by stone. She digs until she reaches a slab that will not lift. She tries anyway. Then she stops trying.

She kneels in the rubble and is fourteen years old.

Then she stacks back the stones she has lifted. Flat, one on the other, neatly. We know these stones. This is how they lie at the burial ground — against the dogs.

She goes. After a few seconds. The pigeons come back, one after another, onto the ledges, into the window holes.



# 30. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq VI "The Envelope" (F30)

.F30 — HIS OWN RESOURCEFULNESS [[orange]]

= "His Own Resourcefulness" · [F30 · The Fields Seq VI]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, KITCHEN — EVENING

Lenz at the table, the glass, the still dripping. A knock.

Not at the door — at the window, from the street. When he looks, there is nobody there any more. But on the windowsill, outside, leaning against the pane: an ENVELOPE.

He brings it in. No sender. His name on it, in a hand he doesn't know.

He opens it.

First the slip. An address. Nothing else — no explanation, no name. Only a street, a house, a floor.

He doesn't understand.

Then the sheets. REPORT. He reads the name on it. He reads the sentence that is underlined. And then — because he is the man he is — he reads the whole thing, slowly, with his finger on the line, the way a field worker reads.

"...the effect considerably stronger than assumed... the pressure in the boiler rises above the values determined... a modification is no longer possible at the current stage of construction... The design will hold."

He reads it twice.

And then he looks at the address. And suddenly he understands it after all.

He folds the sheets. He puts them away. And he doesn't drink. For the first time in four years the glass stands full on the table and he doesn't touch it — because all at once he has something he wants to think about sober.



# 31. GREY · TAU, Seq 7 "The Child of the Lie" (G69–G70)

.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Mayor" · [G69 · TAU Seq 7]

Another day. Jakob is out.

When Anna knocks, the two cups are already standing on the table.

Lenz has grown thinner; it is going fast now. On his neck, above the collar: a new mark.

He sees Anna's gaze — the gaze that always falls on marks, she cannot switch it off. He points his thumb at it, with the precision of an expert:

LENZ
This one's new. I call him the mayor. Sits at the top and does nothing.

His laugh is dry as kindling.

Anna looks closer. Under the collar, half hidden: another one.

ANNA
There's another one under your chin.

LENZ
The deputy.

Anna laughs through her nose. Lenz looks at her with that hungry gaze, and for a moment these are two people who are doing well.

The clock on him is running. We can see it now with the naked eye.


.EXT. WASTELAND / BURIAL GROUND — DAY [[blue]]

= "Anton" · [G70 · TAU Seq 7]

Ruth's stone. Jakob crouches in front of it, straightening what has not slipped: the flat stone, the wooden marker. Work that is none. Anna stands beside him.

For a long time, nothing.

ANNA
Who is Anton?

Jakob moves the stone a finger's width. And back again.

JAKOB
My brother. Never met him.
Died back then, in the night, with the others. He was two. That's all I know.

ANNA
Hm. Weird.

JAKOB
Mama doesn't mean it badly.

Anna looks at him.

Anna sits down on the remnant of wall beside the grave. And pays back:

ANNA
I don't know who mine were. Parents.
(pause)
I have voices. Whispering, nothing more. One word that was loud — once. And a story that keeps coming back and somehow stops.

JAKOB
Will you tell it to me sometime?

ANNA
(after a while)
Perhaps.

They stay until it slowly grows dark.



# 32. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq VI "The Envelope" (F31–F31a)

.F31 — THE TEST [[orange]]

= "The Test" · [F31 · The Fields Seq VI]

EXT. STREET, OUTSIDE THE HOUSE — EVENING

A street like a hundred others. A house, a floor, a door.

Lenz stands on the other side, in a doorway, like a man waiting for nobody. He hasn't brought the slip. He doesn't need it; the address has been in him since he read it.

For a long time nothing happens.

Then the door opens — a crack. A woman steps out, coat, scarf, long sleeves, like everyone. As she steps out she turns and speaks back through the crack into the flat, quietly; we are too far away to hear. It isn't much. It is what you say when someone is staying inside.

She pulls the door shut and goes down the street, past Lenz, without seeing him.

Lenz sees her face.

He doesn't move. He stands like a man who has seen a ghost. She goes round the corner. He is still standing there.

Then his knees give way. He slides down the wall of the doorway until he is sitting on the step, and stays like that, his hands over his face.


.F31a — THE RUIN [[orange]]

= "The Ruin" · [F31a · The Fields Seq VI]

EXT. RUIN BY THE RIVER — NIGHT

He has walked without knowing where. Now he is standing in front of it.

The works. Huge, burst, black against the night sky. For four years he has avoided the way here. Above the main gate, three metres high, hang the letters — still all of them, back then; the rust hasn't taken them yet.

JAHN WORKS.

He looks at it. For a long time.

Then he climbs over the rubble in front of the gate, lump by lump, until he is close enough. He bends down, picks up a stone, weighs it in his hand — and throws. With everything he has.

The stone hits the wall and drops. A small sound against a very large wall. Nothing else. The letters stay where they are.

He sinks to the ground, in the middle of the rubble, and weeps.

He goes home.



# 33. GREY · TAU, Seq 7 "The Child of the Lie" (G71–G77)

.INT. WORKSHOP FLAT — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Chairs" · [G71 · TAU Seq 7]

Another day, the same water he does not drink. The routines grow darker; they slide closer to something.

LENZ
I once set up chairs in a hall. A whole night. Four hundred chairs. For an assembly, in the morning — it was to be the celebration —
(he aligns invisible rows of chairs)
Chairs, child. I hauled chairs for the future. And then..

Break-off.

But nothing more comes. Lenz struggles for composure. Then, out of nowhere:

LENZ
How old are you?

ANNA
Fourteen.

Lenz nods.


Then he gets up, puts away the cup.


.INT. ATTIC — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "Sentences Side by Side" · [G72 · TAU Seq 7]

Anna lies awake, eyes open, the skylight a grey rectangle.

And the sentences come, repeating. As voices, quiet, out of the dark — in her head, with us as witnesses:

RUTH (O.S.)
Anna, let me look at you...

LENZ (O.S.)
... she liked his girl better. The two of them always had something to laugh about ...

MOTHER (O.S.)
... a place, high above everything ...

LENZ (O.S.)
How old are you?

Silence.

Anna sits up.

She looks to the skylight. Sets out.


.EXT./INT. THE HOME, ARCHIVE WINDOW — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "I Have Tea" · [G73 · TAU Seq 7]

The home at night, from outside: grown darker. Somewhere a little light is burning.

Anna climbs. The archive window she knows from before: the latch that does not quite close.

She slips inside —

In the archive room a light is burning.

THE PENCIL sits at the small table between the shelves. In front of him: a pot, two cups.

Two.

@THE PENCIL
I have tea.

As if that were an explanation.

Anna stands at the window, half in, half out.

She comes all the way in.

He pours. The pot does not tremble.

@THE PENCIL
"Tea is the only drink that makes time stand still."
(pause)
That was in a book. The book has been burned for warmth. The sentence has not. Hello, Anna.

ANNA
(taken aback)
Hello.

Anna drinks. It is the best thing anyone has offered her in weeks.

Anna looks around. In one corner the Pencil has set up a makeshift place to sleep. A candle, a few books.

@THE PENCIL
I always cook for two.


.INT. ARCHIVE — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Little Horse" · [G74 · TAU Seq 7]

Shelves, files, the remains of an administration. Anna stands at the table.

ANNA
My intake. There has to be something. A paper. Anything.

The Pencil looks at her. Then he gets up — and fetches no paper.

From the lowest shelf, behind the files: a small bundle of cloth, tied, ten years of dust in its folds.

He lays it in front of her and unwraps it, carefully, with his long grey fingers.

Inside lies a WOODEN LITTLE HORSE.

Small, smooth to the hand, the mane almost loved away — a thing that has spent a thousand hours in a child's hand.

@THE PENCIL
This was with you when you came.

Anna does not move.

ANNA
Who brought me?

@THE PENCIL
That I don't know. Not anymore, probably. The horse I hid back then, just in case. ...


It fits her hand as if the hand had waited ten years for nothing else. Her thumb finds a spot on the mane by itself.

The notebook in her pocket grows heavy as a millstone. She does not reach for it.

We understand one second before her, and then she understands.


.INT. — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Night, Third Time" · [G75 · TAU Seq 7]

THE NIGHT, A THIRD TIME. The strangest:

The room is almost empty. The furniture wrong, too little of it, in the wrong places. Where the vial stood: nothing.

And the voices are exchanged — the woman's voice, whispering, says the man's words; the man's voice is a sound without words.

In the hand of the child under the table lies, matter-of-factly, as if it had always been there:

the little horse.

(Was it there? Was it ever there?)

A KNOCK. The bolt. The door.

And the father's loud word — ten years long nothing but sound — is for the first time almost language: two syllables.

Two syllables that could be "Lorenz." Or anything else.

Whether memory finally releases the word or is only now building it cannot be decided. We have just seen what it can do with a little horse.

The arm stretches into the light. The gun. The mark. Hyper-sharp — the only thing that always stays the same.

And we no longer believe it a single image.


.EXT. ROOF OF THE HOME — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Overlay" · [G76 · TAU Seq 7]

Anna sits on the ridge of the home, where she sat as a child. Below her the dark house, before her the darker city.

The little horse in her fist. The notebook on her knees.

Anna's hand lies on the first page of the notebook.

She does not open it.


.INT. CLASSROOM, THE HOME — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Fourth Face" · [G77 · TAU Seq 7]

The way down from the roof leads through the house. A corridor, an open door: the classroom. Moonlight on empty benches.

Anna means to pass. And goes in.

On the wall the chart still hangs. The works, proud, seen from below. In front of it, small, the four people with the flags.

Anna has looked at this picture for a thousand hours and never seen it.

Three faces are scratched out, the grey shiny with years. The fourth nobody has touched: a young woman, laughing, a child on her arm.

Anna knows the face. She last saw it when there was less of it left.

Ruth.

And next to her, one of the scratched-out ones: the face is gone, the bearing has stayed. She stands turned a little, and one hand lies on the dress, low, the way hands lie when they are guarding something.

Anna stands before the picture for a long time and strokes the woman's belly.



# 34. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq VI "The Envelope" (F32–F33a)

.F32 — AT THE BEDSIDE [[orange]]

= "At the Bedside" · [F32 · The Fields Seq VI]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, BEDROOM — NIGHT

Ruth is asleep. She sleeps a lot now.
He sits down beside her. He has the sheets in his jacket. He has made up his mind to tell her. She is the only one he could tell; she was there, back then, in the kitchen, when the friend was still the friend.

RUTH
(half awake)
Is that you.

LENZ
It's me.

RUTH
The little one?

LENZ
Asleep.

She reaches for his hand. Finds it. Keeps it.

RUTH
You don't smell of — not tonight.

LENZ
No.

RUTH
Good.
(she closes her eyes again)
Then talk to me. When you're sober you never talk.

He looks at her. He opens his mouth. The words are there: He's alive. He knew. I have the address.

They don't come out.

He looks at her arms. At what the night did to her back then and what it goes on doing to her, one mark more every day.

For four years the sum has had only one side: I was in the hall. I set out the chairs. I was with him instead of with you. — And now on the other side there is a name. A man who knew. Who wrote it soft. Who ran away. And on whose account I was in the hall.

Whoever cannot forgive himself will take any address the guilt can be sent to.

RUTH (CONT'D)
Lenz?

LENZ
Sleep. It's ... nothing.

He strokes her arm, over the marks, very lightly. She falls asleep. He sits until her breathing is calm.


.F33 — THE GUN [[orange]]

= "The Gun" · [F33 · The Fields Seq VI]

INT. KESSLER HOUSE, KITCHEN / HALLWAY — NIGHT

From the drawer under the sideboard, right at the back, under cloths: a WEAPON. Old, from a time before everything. He has never needed it. He weighs it in his hand.

He puts it in his pocket.

He goes through the hallway. Outside the door to the small room he stops. Inside, a three-year-old is sleeping, Jakob.

Lenz looks at him, through the crack of the door. For a long time.

Then he pulls the door to, quietly, the way you close doors behind which someone is sleeping.


.F33a — THE ARCHWAY [[orange]]

= "The Archway" · [F33a · The Fields Seq VI]

EXT. BACKYARDS — NIGHT

He steps out into the night. It has rained; the street shines, his boots get wet at the hems.

A backyard, a passage, the street he has crossed a hundred times.

And there the story loses him.

It doesn't go with him. It stays standing at the corner and watches him go into an archway, and then the archway is empty. Somewhere behind it lies a house, a floor, a door.

Somewhere behind it a turning back would still be possible too. A man who stands at a door and doesn't knock after all. Who puts the weapon back in his pocket and goes home and watches his child sleep.

We don't see it. We see the archway. We don't see him come out again.



# 35. GREY · TAU, Seq 7 "The Child of the Lie" (G78–G80)

.EXT. DISTILLERY, WALL — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Chalk List" · [G78 · TAU Seq 7]

The old distillery: a long brick building, dead chimneys, the smell of back then long gone from the walls.

On the yard wall: NAMES. Written in chalk, one below the other, some weathered, some fresh. A list nobody administers and everybody respects.

JAKOB stands in front of the wall. Alone.

His gaze goes down the names — and catches.

There, in neat, old-fashioned handwriting, with the full name, first and last, fresh:

> LORENZ KESSLER <

Jakob stands very still.


.EXT. OUTSIDE THE WORKSHOP — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Request" · [G79 · TAU Seq 7]

Anna comes — and Jakob is already standing there, outside the door, as if he had not been able to go in.

JAKOB
He put himself on it. Properly. Full name.

Anna understands at once what is meant.

JAKOB
Lorenz Kessler.

Silence.

Something sounds in Anna — two syllables, deep under ten years. Or she is building it in this moment. It cannot be decided, not for her, not for us.

Her face does nothing. That is work, and you can see the work.

Then, for the first time since we have known him, Jakob wants something:

JAKOB
You're quick. You think of things.
Help me get him down off there.

Anna — who has never refused him a request, because he never had one — says nothing.

One second. Two. One second too long.

Jakob looks at her. Sees something he cannot name.

JAKOB
You look at him like your grown-ups.
Like one out of your notebook.


ANNA
Get him down. How. Tell me how.

JAKOB
You think of things everywhere else. With the sugar. With the lists. With your dog you thought of something too —

ANNA
The dog was a toll.

JAKOB
The dog was yours. You didn't even wait for them to take hold of him. You nodded and you walked.

ANNA
And you climbed after me onto every roof, grey as ash, and never said a word. You hid your father from me. I see everything.
(pause)
You take a look at him. He doesn't have long — wall or no wall.
(calm)
And if that is his last wish, then let him go.

JAKOB
Yes. You see everything. And say nothing. You sit with him, you listen to him, for weeks — and the one time I need something, you calculate.

ANNA
I don't calculate —

JAKOB
I watched. One second. You calculated like at the toll.

Anna is silent.

JAKOB
People who only ever take — those I know. The whole city takes. I thought —

He breaks off. He does not say what he thought.

Jakob goes. Anna stays standing and looks after him.


.EXT. OUTSIDE THE WORKSHOP — NIGHT [[blue]]

= "The Spyglass at the Door" · [G80 · TAU Seq 7]

Anna does not come to knock.

Through the window, out of the dark of the alley: inside, the small lamp is burning. Jakob sits by the cot — on which the father now lies, sleeping; for the first time we see Lenz on this bed. Jakob simply sits there. Keeping watch.

Then she steps to the door. From her jacket: the spyglass.

She sets it on the threshold. Centered. Properly.

And goes.



# 36. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq VII "The Door" (F34)

.F34 — THE KNOCK [[orange]]

= "The Knock" · [F34 · The Fields Seq VII]

INT. HIDEOUT, LIVING ROOM — NIGHT

An evening like a hundred before it.

The kerosene lamp stands on the floor, because there it lets the least light through the curtain; it throws a yellow circle.

The parents whisper. As always. We don't understand it; we only hear the sound of two people who have spoken quietly for years and are good at it. Then, for the length of one breath, the mother's whisper becomes words:

HELENE (O.S.)
(whispering)
... high above everything a place. You take the path that turns ...

A KNOCK.

The whispering stops.

In four years there has never been a knock.

A look between the parents.

We see Helene, then her arm and her hand coming down, flat, calm, downward. The camera travels down with it, under the table. We see Anna, who knows the sign and is calm. We stay on her.

The bolt. The door. Everything muffled. Slow motion. A flash of light. We see, blurred, only in fragments, Helene running forward. Another flash. Then nothing more. We stay on Anna a few seconds longer.



# 37. GREY · TAU, Seq 8 "The Run" (G81–G87)

.EXT. DISTILLERY COURTYARD — MORNING [[blue]]

= "The Washing" · "The Writing on the Arms" · "The Perhaps" · "The Little Horse on the Table" · [G81 · TAU Seq 8]

Cold in which you can see breath. Kettles of warm water, carried by VOLUNTEERS — nobody assigns them, they come of their own accord. The dying arrange it themselves.

Steam stands over the courtyard.

The RUNNERS, a dozen, lay down what they have carried: jackets, shirts, things. A TABLE for the things. Nobody guards it.

The volunteers wash those who run today. Arms, face, neck — slow, thorough, without ceremony. It looks like what it is: people washing people.

And with the laying down and the washing there are — arms. Bare arms, everywhere in the courtyard, marks in every script. What the city has shrouded since we have known it is simply here. Nobody points at it. It is the only place where it means nothing.

On the other side of the courtyard: LENZ, with bare arms.

Marks, many, old and new — a whole script. We see him without sleeves for the first time, and it is as if we were seeing a man naked for the first time.

Before him JAKOB. The camera's gaze catches his shoes.

Freshly mended. Seams as fine as only one man makes them.

Jakob says nothing. Lenz says nothing.

Then the father's hands rise. Halfway. At half height they stop and fall back down.

Between the two stands fourteen years of air.

ANNA stands at the edge.

Anna's gaze does what her gaze always does. It falls on the arms.

The steam opens. Two seconds, three: on the inner forearm, among many — a mark in the shape of the horse's head.

Perhaps. Perhaps not. The steam moves back in between.

NO INSERT. NO CLOSENESS. NO MUSIC. We are too far away to swear to it, and we stay too far away.

Anna closes her eyes.

She opens them again. Now she looks at his face. Lenz returns the gaze. Briefly, then back to Jakob.

Again we are on Anna.

Then she walks to the table with the laid-down things. Lays, between the jackets and shirts, the wooden little horse. She simply does it.

The camera stays with her and with the table until she turns away.


.EXT. MAIN STREET — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Procession" · [G82 · TAU Seq 8]

The DRUMS set in, deep, like a second heart.

The procession: the drummers at the front, then the runners, in light clothes, with bare arms. Lenz among them — more upright than we have ever seen him, and not yet upright.

The city stands along the edges, dense. And sings the half-forgotten thing. Closer now, close enough for words — a work song, drawn out and heavy: one line that repeats as if it had to keep a back straight, and a third that answers.

> Early at four, before the sun stands high, <

> early at four, before the sun stands high, <

> I go into the dew, before the dew goes dry. <

Old voices carry the words; the children know them only half and hum the gaps. It is the harvest song. The city sings after its runners what it sang all its life on the way to work.

JAKOB runs the first stretch along the edge, level with his father.

The corner where the street opens into the fields. There the living stop. That is the rule nobody explains.

Jakob stops. The procession runs on. From behind, Anna comes running up.


.EXT. THE FIELDS — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Supernova" · [G83 · TAU Seq 8]

The street becomes a field path, the path becomes a trace, the stalks stand man-high and blossom.

At the edge of the fields the runners begin to run.

Really to run. Like children. Like the mad.

And Lenz runs.

In the running something straightens up in him that we have taken for dead the whole film: the shoulders come back. The stride takes hold. The field worker finds a ground he knows — this ground, there is no other he knows like this.

The stalks close over the first one. Over the second.

Just before the blossoming takes him, Lenz turns his face into the smell — the best nose in the city, in the smell of its life —

and LAUGHS.

Then there is only the field.

The drumming slows. Stops.


.EXT. CITY / PATH TO THE HILL — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Stillness After" · [G84 · TAU Seq 8]

The city is very still.

Anna and Jakob walk side by side. Without touching. Without talking.

Around Jakob's neck, on a new strap, fine seams: the spyglass.

They walk the path up the hill, in switchbacks, along the railing.


.EXT. LOOKOUT — DAWN [[blue]]

= "The Story, Aloud" · [G85 · TAU Seq 8]

The city below is grey and still and complete. The thin line of the barrier. Beyond it: world, simply going on.

ANNA and JAKOB sit side by side at the railing. Not touching. The distance: a hand's width.

For a long time, nothing. Then:

ANNA
Mama always talked about us sitting above the city one day. I think it's here.

Jakob does not look at her. That is his way of listening.

Anna looks at the city. Then she recites it — not the way you tell, the way you recite: a text that has been by heart for ten years.

ANNA
High above everything there is a place. You take the path that turns, always by the railing, and you count the steps. And at the top the whole city lies at your feet, like an open book. And in the evening comes the —

She holds.

Not like someone thinking. Like someone walking a staircase in the dark, one they know — and the step is not there.

Jakob does not ask.

JAKOB
(quietly)
Go on.

ANNA
— and it lays itself over everything, and everything that is broken looks whole from up there. At this place we promised you your life. And whoever has been up there knows: behind the city the world does not end. When you are big, we will go up, we three. And you will count the steps.

Pause.


Jakob nods. Looks at the city, the barrier, the thin line.

JAKOB
Mine kept nothing from the Drops but the condensed milk.

Anna laughs. Quietly, through the nose. She does not lean against him. She only sits a little less alone.

Then Jakob lays his hand on the railing, next to hers.


.EXT. ROAD TO THE BARRIER — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Sign" · [G86 · TAU Seq 8]

A road between fields, toward the thin line on the horizon.

At the roadside, crooked in the earth: an official SIGN from before. The lettering almost gone, eaten by weather:

> RESTRICTED AREA <

> NO ENTRY <

Nobody looks at it.

Two children walk beneath it, each with a bundle, toward the horizon behind which the world simply goes on.


.EXT. THE GANG'S WALL — DAY [[blue]]

= "The Dog" · [G87 · TAU Seq 8]

On a wall, far behind them, at the edge of the territory:

the yellow DOG.

He sits and watches them go, the two shrinking figures between the fields.

He does not run after them.

Whether they get through, we do not see. The hope of a way out weighs more.

BLACK.



# 38. COLOUR · The Fields, Seq VII "The Door" (F36)

.F36 — THE OPEN DOOR [[orange]]

= "The Open Door" · [F36 · The Fields Seq VII]

We are back with Anna under the table and take up her gaze. The light of the kerosene lamp burns; the door to the flat stands open. Credits.



